hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3001","3202 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3002","3203 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Numan ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3003","3204 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3004","3205 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and he said: Fifteen sas of dates, without doubt."
"3005","3206 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to me, he said: I asked al-Zuhri about a man who had intercourse with his wife in the month of Ramadan. He said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, and he mentioned similar to it, except that he did not mention the sas. So what we reported in this hadith has included what is in the first two hadiths, because in it is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do you find a slave? He said: No. He said: Then fast two consecutive months. He said: I cannot. He said: Then feed sixty poor persons? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only ordered him with each kind of these three kinds because he did not find the kind he mentioned to him before it. When the man informed him that he was unable to do any of that, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought an araq containing dates; the mention of the araq and what was of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) handing it to the man and ordering him to give charity - that is what Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated in her hadith with which we began narration. So this hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, is more worthy than it, because there was before what is in the hadith of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, something which Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, memorized and Aishah did not memorize, so it is more worthy for what it added. As for the hadith of Malik and Ibn Jurayj, they are from al-Zuhri upon what we mentioned, and we have clarified the defect in that in what preceded of this chapter. So there is established by what we mentioned of expiation in breaking fast by intercourse in fasting in the month of Ramadan what is in the hadith of Mansur, Ibn Uyaynah, and those who agreed with them, from al-Zuhri, from Humayd, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3006","5 - Chapter on fasting while traveling. 3208 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn al-Hasan, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey, and he saw a crowd and a man shaded over, so he asked: What is this? They said: Fasting. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not of righteousness to fast while traveling."
"3007","3209 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3008","3210 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban narrated to me, he said: Jabir ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man on a journey in the shade of a tree with water being sprinkled upon him, and said: What is the matter with him? They said: Fasting, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Fasting while traveling is not of righteousness, so you must take Allah's concession which He has granted you, and accept it."
"3009","3211 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Musaffa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Harb al-Abrash narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting while traveling is not of righteousness."
"3010","3212 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me that Safwan ibn Abd Allah ibn Safwan informed him, from Umm al-Darda, from Kab ibn Asim al-Ashari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not of righteousness to fast while traveling."
"3011","3213 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Safwan ibn Abd Allah, from Umm al-Darda, from Kab ibn Asim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Fasting while traveling is not of righteousness."
"3012","3214 - Muhammad ibn al-Numan al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Zuhri saying: Safwan ibn Abd Allah informed me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Sufyan said: It was mentioned to me that al-Zuhri used to say, though I did not hear it from him: Laysa min am birr am siyamu fi am safar. Abu Jafar said: So some people went to breaking fast in the month of Ramadan while traveling, and claimed that it is better than fasting, and they argued with these reports in that, until some of them said: Whoever fasts while traveling, his fasting does not suffice him, and he must make it up among his family. And they reported it from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3013","3215 - Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Asim ibn Abd Allah ibn Asim, from Abd Allah ibn Amir, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered a man who fasted while traveling to repeat. And they reported it from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, also."
"3014","3216 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail al-Nahdi narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Karim al-Jazari narrated to us, from Ata, from al-Muharrar ibn Abi Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I fasted Ramadan while traveling, so Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered me to repeat the fasting among my family. And others differed with them in that, and said: If he wishes, he fasts, and if he wishes, he breaks fast, and they did not prefer in that breaking fast over fasting, nor fasting over breaking fast. Among the proof for them against the people of the first view, in what they argued with against them, regarding the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Fasting while traveling is not of righteousness, was that it may bear other than what they carried it upon. It may bear: Fasting while traveling is not of the righteousness which is the most righteous righteousness and the highest ranks of righteousness, though fasting while traveling is righteousness - except that other than it of righteousness is more righteous than it. Just as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The poor person is not the circling beggar whom a date or two dates, a morsel or two morsels, turn away. They said: Then who is the poor person, O Messenger of Allah? He said: The one who is shy to ask, and does not find what suffices him, and is not noticed so is given."
"3015","3217 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us with that, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3016","3218 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3017","3219 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Abu al-Walid, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"3018","3220 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Ibn Thawban narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3019","3221 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So the meaning of his saying: The poor person is not the circling beggar was not upon excluding him from all causes of poverty, but he meant thereby: The poor person of complete poverty is not the circling one, but the poor person of complete poverty is the one who does not ask the people and is not known so is given charity. Likewise his saying: Fasting while traveling is not of righteousness is not upon excluding fasting while traveling from being righteousness, but it is upon the meaning: Fasting while traveling is not of the righteousness which is the most righteous righteousness, because breaking fast there may be more righteous than it when it is intended for strength to meet the enemy, and what resembles that. This is a sound meaning, and it is the most worthy of what the meaning of these reports is carried upon, so that they do not contradict it and others of what has also been reported in this chapter. For indeed:"
"3020","3222 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Makkah in the year of the Conquest in Ramadan, and he fasted until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke fast, and the people broke fast with him. And they used to take the latest, then the latest, of the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3021","3223 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Malik and Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, they said: Ibn Shihab informed us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3022","3224 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Except that he said: Until he came to Usfan."
"3023","3225 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3024","3226 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3025","3227 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Abu Zurah narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, from Ikrimah, the freed slave of Ibn Abbas, he narrated to him, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in the year of the Conquest in Ramadan, and he fasted until he reached al-Kadid, and it reached him that fasting was hard upon the people. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a cup of milk, and held it in his hand until the people saw him while he was upon his mount around him, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drank and broke fast, and he handed it to a man beside him and he drank. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted while traveling and broke fast."
"3026","3228 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled in Ramadan, and fasting became severe upon a man among his Companions, and his mount kept wandering with him under the tree. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed of his matter, and he called for a vessel; when the people saw him with it upon his hand, they broke fast."
"3027","3229 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Makkah in the year of the Conquest in Ramadan, and he fasted until he reached Kura al-Ghamim, and the people fasted with him. It reached him that fasting had become hard upon the people, watching what he did, so he called for a cup of water after Asr, and drank while the people looked on. It reached him that some people fasted afterwards, so he said: Those are the disobedient."
"3028","3230 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih informed me, from Rabiah ibn Yazid, from Qazaah, he said: I asked Abu Said about fasting Ramadan while traveling. He said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Ramadan in the year of the Conquest, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fast and we would fast, until he reached a stop among the stops, and said: You have drawn near to your enemy, and breaking fast is stronger for you. So we woke in the morning, among us fasting and among us breaking fast. Then we traveled and stopped at a stop, and said: You will meet your enemy in the morning, and breaking fast is stronger for you, so break fast. So it was a decisive command from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then I saw myself fasting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before that and after that."
"3029","3231 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to me that Bakr ibn Abd Allah narrated to him, he said: I heard Anas saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey with his Companions, and fasting became hard upon them, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a vessel and drank while upon his mount, with the people looking at him."
"3030","3232 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Sumayy, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, from a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Arj in the heat, pouring water upon his head while fasting, from thirst or from heat. Then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached al-Kadid, he broke fast."
"3031","3233 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Atiyyah ibn Qays narrated to us, from Qazaah ibn Yahya, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when two nights of Ramadan had passed, and we went out fasting until we reached al-Kadid, then he ordered us to break fast, so we woke in the morning, among us fasting and among us breaking fast. When we reached Marr al-Zahran, he informed us of meeting the enemy and ordered us to break fast. Abu Jafar said: So in these reports is establishment of the permissibility of fasting while traveling, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only left it out of care for his Companions. So is it permissible for anyone to say regarding that fasting that it was not righteousness? This is not permissible; rather it is righteousness. And breaking fast may be more righteous than it when strength for meeting the enemy is intended thereby, for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to break fast. For this meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them - and Allah knows best: Fasting while traveling is not of righteousness, upon this meaning which we mentioned. If one says: The breaking fast of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his ordering his Companions with that after his fasting and their fasting which he did not forbid them from, abrogates the ruling of fasting while traveling altogether. It is said to him: What is your proof for what you mentioned? And in the hadith of Abu Said al-Khudri - which we mentioned in the section before this - is that he used to fast with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while traveling after that? So this hadith indicates that fasting while traveling after the breaking fast of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned in these reports is permissible. And Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who is one of those from whom was reported regarding the breaking fast of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we mentioned, said what:"
"3032","Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Malik, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, only intended by breaking fast while traveling facilitation for you; so whoever finds fasting easy, let him fast, and whoever finds breaking fast easy, let him break fast."
"3033","3234 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: If he wishes, he fasts, and if he wishes, he breaks fast. So this Ibn Abbas did not make the breaking fast of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while traveling after his fasting in it an abrogation of fasting while traveling; rather he placed it upon facilitation. If one says: What is the meaning of the saying of Ibn Abbas in the hadith of Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah which you mentioned from him regarding that: And they used to take the latest, then the latest, of the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? It is said to him: Its meaning - according to us, and Allah knows best - is that they did not know before that that the traveler may break fast while traveling, just as he may not break fast while resident. And the ruling of residence and the ruling of travel in that were - according to them - equal, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) introduced for them that act of his which permitted them breaking fast in their journeys, so they took that upon the permissibility for them to break fast, and for them to leave breaking fast. This is the meaning of this hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and what we mentioned from him of his saying which we described indicates that. And there has been mentioned from Anas ibn Malik, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding that, close to what we mentioned from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then there has been reported from Anas what indicates that its meaning according to him is like its meaning which we mentioned from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3034","3235 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim - who is al-Ahwal - he said: I asked Anas ibn Malik about fasting the month of Ramadan while traveling, and he said: Fasting is better."
"3035","3236 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, from Asim, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: If you break fast, it is a concession, and if you fast, fasting is better."
"3036","3237 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Asim narrating from Anas, he said: If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break fast, and fasting is better. And among what the people of the first view also argued with in rejecting fasting while traveling was what we mentioned in other than this place, of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah has lifted fasting from the traveler. They said: Since fasting is lifted from him, when he fasts it, he has fasted it while it is not obligatory upon him, so it does not suffice him. Among the proof for the others against them in that was that it is possible that that fasting which He lifted from him is the fasting from which he has no escape in those days, just as the resident has no escape from that; and in this hadith is what indicates this meaning. Do you not see him saying: And from the pregnant and nursing woman. Do you not see that the pregnant and nursing woman, when they fast Ramadan, that suffices them, and that they are not like one who fasted before fasting became obligatory upon him; rather He made what fasting becomes obligatory upon them by the entry of the month, and made for them its delay due to necessity - and the traveler in that is like them both. And this is the most worthy of what this report is carried upon, so that it does not contradict others of the reports which we mentioned in this chapter. And among the proof against the people of the first view which we mentioned, for the people of the second view which we described, was that we saw them with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after he permitted them breaking fast while traveling, fasting in it. So among what was reported in that is what:"
"3037","3238 - Yazid ibn Sinan, Rabi al-Jizi, and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Sad narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hayyan al-Dimashqi, from Umm al-Darda, she said: Abu al-Darda said: I saw us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in some of his journeys on a day of severe heat, until a man would place his hand upon his head from the severity of heat, and none of us was fasting except the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abd Allah ibn Rawahah."
"3038","3239 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and among us fasting and among us breaking fast, and neither would fault the other."
"3039","3240 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: I heard Qatadah narrating from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest of Makkah, nineteen or seventeen nights of Ramadan (gone); fasting ones fasted and breaking ones broke fast, and neither faulted these nor these those."
"3040","3241 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Twelve nights."
"3041","3242 - Ali narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Eighteen nights."
"3042","3243 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3043","3244 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not mention the conquest of Makkah."
"3044","3245 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Asim, from Muwarriq al-Ijli, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and we stopped on a day of severe heat; among us fasting and among us breaking fast. We stopped on a hot day, and the most shaded of us was the owner of the cloak, and among us was one who shielded the sun with his hand. So the fasting ones fell, and the breaking ones stood, struck the buildings (tents), and watered the mounts. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The breaking ones have taken the reward today."
"3045","3246 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Ramadan, and neither did the fasting fault the breaking, nor the breaking the fasting. So what we mentioned in these reports indicates that what was of the breaking fast of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his ordering his Companions with that is not upon prohibiting fasting while traveling, and that it is upon permitting breaking fast. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he fasted while traveling and broke fast."
"3046","3247 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Salam, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast while traveling and used to break his fast."
"3047","3248 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Al-Hasan ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: Al-Mu'afa ibn Imran narrated to us, from Mughirah ibn Ziyad, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted while traveling and broke his fast. This indicates that the traveler may fast and may break his fast. Hamzah al-Aslami asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about fasting while traveling, and he said to him: If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break your fast."
"3048","3249 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us of that, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id and Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami."
"3049","3250 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, he said: Imran ibn Abi Anas narrated to me, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami, the like of it."
"3050","3251 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Should I fast while traveling? And he was one who fasted much. The Prophet said to him: If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break your fast. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted fasting while traveling for whoever wishes that, and breaking the fast for whoever wishes that. Thus it is established by this and by what we mentioned before it that fasting Ramadan while traveling is permissible. A group held that there is no merit for one who fasts Ramadan while traveling over one who breaks it and makes it up afterward. They said: Neither of the two is better than the other, and they argued for that by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) giving Hamzah ibn Amr the choice between breaking the fast while traveling and fasting, without commanding him to do one rather than the other. Others disagreed with them on that and said: Fasting while traveling in the month of Ramadan is better than breaking the fast. They said to the people of the view we mentioned: There is no proof in what you mentioned of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) giving Hamzah the choice between fasting while traveling and breaking the fast that neither is better than the other; rather he only gave him the choice of what he is allowed to do of breaking the fast and fasting, while we see that the entry of the month of Ramadan makes fasting obligatory upon travelers and residents alike if they are accountable. Since the entry of Ramadan is what obligates fasting upon all of them, whoever among them hastens to perform what is obligatory upon him is better than whoever delays it. Thus it is established by what we mentioned that fasting while traveling is better than breaking the fast, and this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. That is also reported from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, and from a number of the Successors."
"3051","3252 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hammad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Fasting is better, and breaking the fast is a concession, meaning: while traveling."
"3052","3253 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Mujahid, that they said about fasting while traveling: If you wish, fast, and if you wish, break your fast, and fasting is better."
"3053","3254 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Habib narrated to us, from Amr ibn Harim, he said: Jabir ibn Zayd was asked about fasting Ramadan while traveling. He said: Whoever wishes fasts if he is able to do that, so long as he does not undertake a matter that is hard upon him; Allah the Exalted only intended by breaking the fast to make things easy for His servants."
"3054","3255 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr informed us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she used to fast while traveling in the heat. I said: What prompted her to do that? He said: She used to hasten. So this Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, held that hastening to fast Ramadan while traveling is better than delaying that until residence."
"3055","3256 - Yunus, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us. (H). 3257 - And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, both said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Mansur al-Kalbi, that Dihyah ibn Khalifah went out from his village in Damascus to about the distance of the village of Uqbah in Ramadan, and he broke his fast while people were with him, while others disliked breaking their fast. When he returned to his village, he said: By Allah, I have seen today a matter I never thought I would see: a people who turned away from the guidance of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions, saying that of those who fasted, then he said: O Allah, take me to You. Part of the proof for those who preferred fasting while traveling in this hadith is that Dihyah only censured one who turns away from the guidance of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions; so whoever fasts on his journey in that manner is censured, while whoever fasts on his journey without turning away from his guidance, but rather holding fast to his guidance, is praiseworthy."
"3056","3258 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, he said: Abu al-Aswad informed us, that he heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrating, from Abu Murawih al-Aslami, from Hamzah ibn Amr al-Aslami, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: O Messenger of Allah, I fast continuously; should I fast while traveling? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is only a concession from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for His servants; whoever accepts it, it is good and beautiful, and whoever leaves it, there is no sin upon him. He said: Hamzah used to fast perpetually while traveling and while resident, and Abu Murawih was likewise, and Urwah was likewise. What we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicates that fasting while traveling is better than breaking the fast, and that breaking the fast is only a concession."
"3057","3259 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed us, he said: Abu al-Aswad informed us, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to fast perpetually while traveling and while resident."
"3058","Chapter 6 - The fast of the day of Arafah. 3260 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us. (H). And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us. (H). 3261 - And Bakr ibn Idris and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, both said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, they said: Musa ibn Ulayy narrated to us, from his father, from Uqbah. Bakr and Salih said in their hadith: He said: I heard my father narrating, from Uqbah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The days of sacrifice and the days of Tashriq, and the day of Arafah, are a feast day of the people of Islam, days of eating and drinking. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this hadith and thereby disliked fasting the day of Arafah, making its fast like the fast of the day of sacrifice. Others disagreed with them on that and said: There is nothing wrong with fasting the day of Arafah. Part of the proof for them in that is that it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only intended by his prohibition of fasting the day of Arafah those standing at Arafat, because it is a feast there, and it is not so elsewhere; Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, clarified that."
"3059","3262 - Muhammad ibn Idris al-Makki and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us. (H). 3263 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, both said: Hawshab ibn Aqil narrated to us, from Mahdi al-Hajari, from Ikrimah, he said: We were with Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in his house, and he narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting the day of Arafah at Arafat. So Abu Hurayrah informed that the prohibition from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of fasting the day of Arafah applies only at Arafat specifically. The people of the first view also argued for their view by what:"
"3060","3264 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not fast, nor did Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman or Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, the day of Arafah. It was said to them: This too, in our view, concerns fasting the day of Arafah at the standing-place, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, clarified that in other than this hadith."
"3061","3265 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah and Abu Dawud narrated to us, both said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from a man, that a man asked Ibn Umar about fasting the day of Arafah at the standing-place, and he said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he did not fast it, and with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he did not fast it, and with Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he did not fast it, and with Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and he did not fast it, and I do not fast it, nor do I command you nor forbid you; if you wish then do not fast it. This hadith clarifies that what Nafi' reported from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, concerns fasting at the standing-place."
"3062","3266 - It is reported from Ibn Umar about commanding the fast of the day of Arafah what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, he said: Raqabah narrated to us, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, he said: I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, asked about fasting Friday and the day of Arafah, and he commanded fasting them both. It is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the reward of fasting the day of Arafah, in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, what has:"
"3063","3267 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghaylan ibn Jarir narrating, from Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about fasting the day of Arafah, and he said: It expiates the past year and the remaining year."
"3064","3268 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghaylan ibn Jarir narrating, from Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani, from Abu Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I expect from Allah regarding the fast of the day of Arafah that it expiates the year before it and the year after it."
"3065","3269 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, he said: He read to al-Fudayl, he said: Abu Hariz narrated to me, that he heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr saying: A man asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, about fasting the day of Arafah. He said: When we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we deemed it equal to fasting a year. Thus it is established by this report, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the encouragement to fast the day of Arafah. This indicates that what was disliked of fasting it in the first reports is due to the circumstance we mentioned of standing at Arafat, because of the severity of their exertion. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3066","Chapter 7 - The fast of the day of Ashura. 3270 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Habib ibn Hind ibn Asma, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to my people of Aslam and said: Say to them: Let them fast the day of Ashura; whomever you find among them who has eaten from the beginning of his day, let him fast the rest of it."
"3067","3271 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Salamah al-Khuza'i - he is Abu al-Minhal - from his uncle, he said: We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the morning of the day of Ashura, having already eaten breakfast, and he said: Did you fast this day? We said: We have already eaten breakfast. He said: Then complete the rest of your day."
"3068","3272 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to me, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Abu al-Minhal narrating from his uncle, who was of Aslam, that some people came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Ashura, and he said: Did you fast today? They said: No, and we have eaten. He said: Then fast the rest of your day. Abu Ja'far said: In these reports is the obligatoriness of fasting the day of Ashura, and in the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding them to fast it after they had begun the morning is proof that one who is in a day whose fast is incumbent upon him specifically, and who had not intended its fast from the night, it suffices him to intend its fast after morning, if that is before midday, according to what the people of knowledge said about that. There is reported about fasting the day of Ashura more than what we mentioned."
"3069","3273 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Dhakwan narrated to us, from al-Rubayyi' bint Mu'awwidh, he said: I asked her about fasting the day of Ashura. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent word to the towns: Whoever began the morning fasting, let him remain upon his fast, and whoever began the morning not fasting, let him complete the rest of his day. We continued to fast it afterward and to make our young children fast it while they were small, and we would make them a toy of wool; when they asked us for food we gave them the toy. In this hadith is that they would withhold food from their children and make them fast the day of Ashura. This, in our view, is not permissible, because children are not obligated to fast, pray, or do anything else. How could they be obligated with anything of that when Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has lifted the pen from them?"
"3070","3274 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, from Abdullah ibn Abbas, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The pen is lifted from three: from the child until he grows up, from the sleeper until he wakes, and from the insane until he recovers."
"3071","3275 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Sound reports are reported about the abrogation of fasting the day of Ashura from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3072","3276 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Al-Mubarak ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Isma'il, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, he said: I entered upon Ibn Mas'ud on the day of Ashura while he had fresh dates with him, and he said: Come near. I said: This is the day of Ashura and I am fasting. He said: This day we were commanded to fast before Ramadan."
"3073","3277 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from his father, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Qays ibn al-Sakan, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: A man came to him while he was eating and he said to him: Come. He said: I am fasting. Abdullah said to him: We used to fast it, then it was abandoned, meaning: the day of Ashura."
"3074","3278 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded fasting the day of Ashura before Ramadan was obligated; when Ramadan was obligated he said: Whoever wishes fasts Ashura, and whoever wishes breaks his fast."
"3075","3279 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad and Shu'ayb narrated to us, both said: Al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me that Irak informed him that Urwah informed him, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3076","3280 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from al-Ash'ath, from Ja'far ibn Abi Thawr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us to fast Ashura and urge us upon it and check upon us regarding it; when Ramadan was obligated, he did not command us, did not forbid us, and did not check upon us regarding it."
"3077","3281 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: I heard Shu'bah, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abu Ammar, from Qays ibn Sa'd ibn Ubadah, he said: We were commanded to fast Ashura before Ramadan was obligated; when Ramadan was obligated we were neither commanded nor forbidden regarding it, and we do it."
"3078","3282 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hakam, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Qays ibn Sa'd, the like of it."
"3079","3283 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. In these reports is the abrogation of the obligatoriness of fasting the day of Ashura, and proof that its fast was returned to voluntary after having been obligatory. Other reports are reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which there is proof that its fast was by choice, not obligatory. Among them is what:"
"3080","3284 - Abu Bakrah and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, both said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Medina, he found the Jews fasting the day of Ashura, so he asked them, and they said: This is the day in which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, gave Moses, peace be upon him, victory over Pharaoh. He said: You are more entitled to Moses than they are, so fast it. In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only fasted it in gratitude to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for giving Moses victory over Pharaoh; that is by choice, not as an obligation."
"3081","3285 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid narrated to us, that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: I do not know the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to single out fasting any day over another except this day, the day of Ashura, or the month of Ramadan."
"3082","3286 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Azraqi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ward narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah saying: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: No day has merit over a day in fasting except the month of Ramadan and the day of Ashura."
"3083","3287 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hajib ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hakam ibn al-A'raj saying: I said to Ibn Abbas: Inform me about the day of Ashura. He said: About which of its aspects do you ask? I said: I ask about fasting it, which day do I fast? He said: When you wake on the ninth, wake fasting. I said: Did Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fast so? He said: Yes. This Ibn Abbas is reported from, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to fast the day of Ashura. That indicates his fasting it was by choice, not as an obligation, along with what Sa'id ibn Jubayr reported from Ibn Abbas in informing of the reason for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fasted that day."
"3084","3288 - Al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Jabir, from Sa'd ibn Ubaydah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast the day of Ashura. It is possible that this too is for the reason Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, mentioned."
"3085","3289 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Thuwayr, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr saying: This is the day of Ashura, so fast it; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command fasting it. It is possible that this is for the reason we mentioned as well."
"3086","3290 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Maysarah al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Mazidah ibn Jabir narrated to us, from his mother, that Uthman appointed Abu Musa over Kufah, and he said on the day of Ashura: Fast this day; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast it. This hadith bears the same meaning as in the hadith of Ibn Abbas as well."
"3087","3291 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Hurr ibn al-Sayyah, from Hunaydah ibn Khalid, from his wife, from one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast nine days of Dhu al-Hijjah, the day of Ashura, and three days of every month. This too is like the one before it."
"3088","3292 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umays narrated to us, from Qays ibn Muslim, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Abu Musa, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The day of Ashura was a day the Jews used to fast and take as a feast, so fast it yourselves. In this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded fasting it because the Jews used to fast it. Ibn Abbas informed in his hadith of the reason for which the Jews fasted it, that it was in gratitude to Allah the Exalted for giving Moses victory over Pharaoh, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) likewise fasted it; fasting for gratitude is by choice, not obligatory."
"3089","3293 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Umar and al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever among you wishes to fast the day of Ashura, let him fast it, and whoever does not wish, let him leave it."
"3090","3294 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying about the day of Ashura: This is a day Quraysh used to fast in the pre-Islamic period; whoever wishes to fast it, let him fast it, and whoever wishes to leave it, let him leave it."
"3091","3295 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghaylan ibn Jarir narrating from Abdullah ibn Ma'bad, from Abu Qatadah - I said: al-Ansari? He said: al-Ansari - from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said about fasting the day of Ashura: I expect from Allah that it expiates the year before it."
"3092","3296 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghaylan, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3093","3297 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ghaylan, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. In this hadith is that he commanded them to fast it in expectation of what he mentioned of expiation; this, in our view, does not contradict the hadith of Ibn Abbas, for it is possible that he fasted it in gratitude to Allah when He gave Moses victory over Pharaoh, so He thanks Allah with it, and thereby expiates the past year."
"3094","3298 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, both said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he heard Mu'awiyah in the year he performed Hajj, while upon the pulpit, saying: O people of Medina, where are your scholars? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying about this day: This is the day of Ashura, and its fast has not been prescribed upon you, while I am fasting; so whoever wishes, let him fast, and whoever wishes, let him break his fast. It is possible that by his saying: Its fast has not been prescribed upon you, he meant: the fast of that day in that year. This does not negate that it may have been prescribed upon them in years before that year, then abrogated afterward as in the first hadiths. Thus the abrogation of the fast of the day of Ashura which was obligatory is established, and the command of it by choice, and he informed of the reward in that; so fasting it is good, and it is the tenth day - Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that in the hadith of al-Hakam ibn al-A'raj, and he mentioned that also from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3095","3299 - It is also reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Abbas, from Abdullah ibn Umayr, from Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: If I live to next year, I shall fast the ninth day, meaning Ashura."
"3096","3300 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir and Abu Dawud narrated to us, both said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: I shall fast Ashura, the ninth day."
"3097","3301 - Ibn Marzuq and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, both said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Sulayman. His saying: I shall fast Ashura, the ninth day, is information from him that that day will be the day of Ashura, and his saying: I shall fast the ninth day, bears the meaning: I shall fast the ninth along with the tenth, so that I do not single out the day of Ashura for fasting as the Jews do, but rather mix it with another, so I have fasted it differently from how the Jews fast it. It is reported from Ibn Abbas what indicates this meaning."
"3098","3302 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ata informed me that he heard Ibn Abbas saying: Differ from the Jews, and fast the ninth and the tenth. This indicates that Ibn Abbas understood the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): If I live to next year I shall fast the ninth day, as we understood it. There also came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that what:"
"3099","3303 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from Dawud ibn Ali, from his father, from his grandfather Ibn Abbas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about fasting the day of Ashura: Fast it, and fast a day before it or a day after it, and do not resemble the Jews."
"3100","3304 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Thus it is established by this hadith what we mentioned: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only intended by fasting the ninth to include the fast of the day of Ashura with other fasting, so that it is not singled out for fasting by itself, as came from him about fasting Friday."
"3101","3305 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman informed us, from Sa'id - he is Ibn Abi Arubah - from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon Juwayriyah, may Allah be pleased with her, on Friday while she was fasting. He said to her: Did you fast yesterday? She said: No. He said: Will you fast tomorrow? She said: No. He said: Then break your fast."
"3102","3306 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: I heard Abu Ayyub al-Ataki narrating from Juwayriyah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon her, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3103","3307 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah, Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3104","3308 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not fast Friday unless you fast a day before it or a day after it."
"3105","3309 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us, he said: I heard a man of Banu al-Harith ibn Ka'b narrating from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of its meaning."
"3106","3310 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Ziyad al-Harithi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3107","3311 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Qasim ibn Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I asked al-Hasan about fasting Friday, and he said: It is forbidden except in consecutive days. 3312 - Then he said: Abu Rafi' narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting Friday except in days before it or after it."
"3108","3313 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us that Abu al-Khayr narrated to him that Hudhayfah al-Bariqi narrated to him that Junadah ibn Abi Umayyah al-Azdi narrated to him that they entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a Friday, and he brought food near to them and said: Eat. They said: We are fasting. He said: Did you fast yesterday? They said: No. He said: Will you fast tomorrow? They said: No. He said: Then break your fast."
"3109","3314 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abu Bishr, from Amir ibn Ludayn al-Ash'ari, that he asked Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, about fasting Friday, and he said: You have come upon one well-informed; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Friday is your feast, so do not make your feast day your fast day unless you fast before it or after it. Just as it is disliked to single out Friday by itself for fasting unless it is joined with a day before it or a day after it, so that it enters into a fast and becomes part of it, likewise, in our view, all other days: one should not single out a day among them for fasting, just as one should not single out the day of Ashura or Friday for themselves. Rather one intends fasting on whatever days it falls. What is meant by the dislike we described is to distinguish between the month of Ramadan and all else people fast besides it, because the month of Ramadan is singled out for fasting as a specific month, since Allah's, Mighty and Majestic, obligation upon His servants is to fast it specifically, except those excused among them by illness, travel or other, while other months are not so. This is the explanation of what is reported about fasting the day of Ashura from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); we have clarified and explained it in this chapter."
"3110","Chapter 8 - The fast of Saturday. 3315 - Ibn Marzuq - he is Ibrahim - narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Abdullah ibn Busr, from his sister al-Samma, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Do not fast Saturday in other than what is obligated upon you, even if none of you finds but the bark of a tree or a grape stalk, let her chew it. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this hadith and disliked fasting Saturday voluntarily. Others disagreed with them and saw nothing wrong with fasting it. Part of the proof against them in that is that a hadith came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade fasting Friday unless a day before it or a day after it is fasted. We mentioned that with its chains in what preceded of this book of ours; the day after it is Saturday. In these reported reports about this is the permissibility of fasting Saturday voluntarily, and they are more famous and more manifest in the hands of scholars than this isolated hadith which contradicts them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted fasting Ashura and urged it, and did not say: If it is Saturday then do not fast it. In that is proof that all days enter into it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved fast to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is the fast of Dawud, peace be upon him; he used to fast a day and break a day, and we shall mention that with its chain in its place in this book of ours, if Allah the Exalted wills. In that too is the equal treatment of Saturday and all other days. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also commanded fasting the white days, and there is reported from him about that what:"
"3111","3316 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman and Hakim, from Musa ibn Talhah, from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, from Abu Dharr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man, commanding him to fast the thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth."
"3112","3317 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Hibban narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Qatadah ibn Milhan al-Qaysi, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command us to fast the white nights: the thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth, and he said: They are like the fast of perpetuity. Saturday may fall within these, as other days among all days fall within them. In them too is the permissibility of fasting Saturday voluntarily. Al-Zuhri rejected the hadith of al-Samma about the dislike of fasting Saturday, and did not count it among the hadith of the people of knowledge, after his knowledge of it."
"3113","3318 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Al-Zuhri was asked about fasting Saturday, and he said: There is nothing wrong with it. It was said to him: It is reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about its dislike, and he said: That is a Himsi hadith; al-Zuhri did not count it as a hadith to be acted upon, and he weakened it. It is possible in our view, and Allah knows best, if it is authentic, that fasting it was only forbidden lest it be venerated thereby, abstaining from food, drink and intercourse in it as the Jews do. As for one who fasts it without intending its veneration, nor what the Jews intend by abandoning work in it, that is not disliked. If one says: He permitted fasting specific days singled out for fasting, namely the white days; this proves there is nothing wrong with singling out a day by itself for fasting. It is said to him: It is said that the white days were only commanded to be fasted because eclipses occur in them and not in others, and we are commanded to draw near to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, by prayer, manumission and other righteous deeds at eclipses. So fasting these days was commanded so that it be righteousness performed after an eclipse; that is fasting not singled out for a day by itself. Rather it is fasting intended at a time in gratitude to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for an occurrence in it; there is nothing wrong with that. Likewise Friday, if a man fasts it in gratitude for an occurrence, such as a solar or lunar eclipse, or in gratitude to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, there is nothing wrong with that even if he does not fast a day before it nor after it."
"3114","Chapter 9 - Fasting after the middle of Sha'ban until Ramadan. 3319 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Hibban and Ya'qub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, both said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim al-Qass narrated to us, he said: Al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no fast after the middle of Sha'ban until Ramadan. Abu Ja'far said: A group held the dislike of fasting after the middle of Sha'ban until Ramadan, and argued for that by this hadith. Others disagreed with them and said: There is nothing wrong with fasting all of Sha'ban; it is good, not forbidden. They argued for that by what:"
"3115","3320 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to me, from Layth, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to join Sha'ban with Ramadan."
"3116","3321 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Salim, from Abu Salamah, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fast two consecutive months except Sha'ban and Ramadan."
"3117","3322 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ghusn Thabit ibn Qays narrated to us, from Abu Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Usamah ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to fast two days of every week without leaving them. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I see you never leave fasting two days of every week. He said: Which two days? I said: Monday and Thursday. He said: Those are two days in which deeds are presented to the Lord of the worlds, and I love that my deed be presented while I am fasting."
"3118","3323 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. He added: He said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fast of any month what he fasts of Sha'ban. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I see you fast of Sha'ban what you do not fast of other months. He said: It is a month people neglect, between Rajab and Ramadan, and it is a month in which deeds are raised to the Lord of the worlds, and I love that my deed be raised while I am fasting."
"3119","3324 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Nafi' ibn Yazid informed us that Ibn al-Had narrated to him that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to him, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never fasted in any month what he fasted in Sha'ban; he used to fast it all except a little, indeed he used to fast it all."
"3120","3325 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never fasted in the year more than his fasting in Sha'ban; he used to fast it all."
"3121","3326 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr informed us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me, and he mentioned the like of it."
"3122","3327 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from Abu Salamah, he said: I asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about the fasting of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: He used to fast until we would say: He does not break his fast, and break his fast until we would say: He does not fast; and he used to fast Sha'ban, or most of Sha'ban."
"3123","3328 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Yazid al-Rishk narrated to us, from Mu'adhah al-Adawiyyah, she said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was asked: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fast three days of every month? She said: Yes. It was said to her: Of which part? She said: He did not care of which part of the month he fasted them. They said: In these reports is proof that there is nothing wrong with fasting all of Sha'ban. Part of the proof of the first ones against them was that what is reported in these reports is only information about the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while what precedes that containing the prohibition is information about his saying; both hadiths should be authenticated. So what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did was made permissible for him, and what he forbade was made prohibited for others, so the ruling of others in that is opposite his ruling, until both hadiths are sound and do not contradict. Part of the proof against them in that was that in the hadith of Usamah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said about Sha'ban: It is a month people neglect fasting. This indicates that their fasting it is better than breaking the fast. There is also reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates what we mentioned."
"3124","3329 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Musa narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best fast after Ramadan is Sha'ban."
"3125","3330 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn Musa, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Which fast is best? Meaning after Ramadan. He said: The fast of Sha'ban in veneration of Ramadan."
"3126","3331 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from Thabit, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Imran ibn Husayn, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: Did you fast of the end of Sha'ban? He said: No. He said: When you break Ramadan then fast two days."
"3127","3332 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu al-Ala, from Mutarrif ibn Abdullah - he is Ibn al-Shikhkhir - from Imran, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it, except that he said: Fast a day. Abu Ja'far said: This is at the end of Sha'ban; in these reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding his community is what agrees with his act. There is also reported from him about that what:"
"3128","3333 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not precede Ramadan by fasting a day or two days, except that it be a man who used to fast a fast, then let him fast it."
"3129","3334 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3130","3335 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, and he mentioned the like of it."
"3131","3336 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3132","3337 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim and Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Yahya, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3133","3338 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Wuhazi - meaning Yahya ibn Salih - narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3134","3339 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Unless that coincides with a fast one of you used to fast, then let him fast it, this indicates the rebuttal of what the people of the first view said, and that the ruling of fasting what is after the middle of Sha'ban until Ramadan is the ruling of fasting all other time whose fasting is permissible. When this meaning we mentioned is established, this indicates that the prohibition which came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, was only out of his concern for those fasting Ramadan, not for any other reason. Likewise we command whoever fasting near Ramadan brings upon him weakness preventing him from fasting Ramadan not to fast until he fasts Ramadan, because fasting Ramadan is more deserving for him than fasting what is not obligatory upon him. This is the meaning the meaning of that hadith should be carried upon, so that it does not contradict the other of these hadiths. There is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in what he commanded Abdullah ibn Amr, what indicates that as well."
"3135","3340 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Amr ibn Aws - a man of Thaqif - from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved fast to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is the fast of Dawud; he used to fast a day and break a day."
"3136","3341 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us. (H). 3342 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, both said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn al-Fayyad, he said: I heard Abu Iyad, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3137","3343 - Abu Bakrah and Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, both said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Amr ibn Aws informed him, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most beloved fast to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is the fast of Dawud, and he used to fast half of time."
"3138","3344 - Ibn Marzuq - meaning Ibrahim - narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Thabit narrated to us, from Shu'ayb ibn Abdullah ibn Amr, from Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning - and asked him about fasting, and he said to him: Fast a day, and you have ten days. He said: Increase me, O Messenger; for I have strength. He said: Fast two days and you have nine days. He said: Increase me, O Messenger of Allah; for I have strength. He said: Fast three days, and you have eight days."
"3139","3345 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: It suffices you to fast of every month three days; for every good deed ten like it, so that is the fast of all time. I was strict upon myself so it was made strict upon me. I said: I can do other than that, more than that. He said: Fast the fast of Allah's Prophet Dawud. I said: What is the fast of Dawud, Allah's Prophet? He said: Half of time."
"3140","3346 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Yahya narrated to me, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3141","3347 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: It reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that I say: I shall fast perpetually. He said: Fast three days of every month. I said: I can do better than that. He said: Fast a day and break two days. I said: I can do better than that. He said: Then fast a day and break a day; that is the fast of Dawud, and it is the most just fast."
"3142","3348 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, that Sa'id informed him and Abu Salamah, that Abdullah ibn Amr said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed, and he mentioned the like of it."
"3143","3349 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3144","3350 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb and Rawh said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Talhah ibn Hilal - or: Hilal ibn Talhah - he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Abdullah, fast three days of every month; whoever brings a good deed has ten like it. I said: I can do more than that. He said: Fast the fast of Dawud; he used to fast a day and break a day."
"3145","3351 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha narrated to us, he said: Abu Qilabah narrated to me, he said: Abu al-Malih narrated to me, he said: I entered with your father Zayd ibn Amr upon Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, and he narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was told of his fasting. He said: He came to me and I laid for him a cushion of leather stuffed with fiber, and he sat on the ground and said to me: Three days of every month suffice you. I said: O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then five days. I said: O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then seven days. I said: O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then nine days. I said: O Messenger of Allah. He said: Then eleven days. I said: O Messenger of Allah. He said: I think he said: Thirteen days. I said: O Messenger of Allah. He said: There is no fast above the fast of Dawud: half of time, fasting a day and breaking a day."
"3146","3352 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How do you fast? I said: I fast and do not break. He said: Fast of every month three days. I said: I am stronger than that. He continued decreasing with me and I with him until he said: Then fast the most beloved fast to Allah, Mighty and Majestic: the fast of Dawud: fasting a day and breaking a day."
"3147","3353 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu al-Abbas, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have I not been told that you fast perpetually and stand the night? He said: I said: I am able. He said: If you do, the soul becomes weary and the eye grows dim. He said: I said: I am able. He said: Then fast three days of every month. He said: I said: I am able. He said: Then fast the fast of my brother Dawud; he used to fast a day and break a day, and would not flee when he met the enemy."
"3148","3354 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, he said: I heard Abu al-Abbas - a man of the people of Mecca, who was a poet and was not suspected in hadith - he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr, and he mentioned the like of it."
"3149","3355 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Surayj narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn and Mughirah informed us, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Fast of every month three days, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3150","3356 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: I heard Ghaylan ibn Jarir narrating, from Abdullah ibn Ma'bad al-Zimmani, from Abu Qatadah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about one who fasts a day and breaks a day. He said: That is the fast of Dawud. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what of one who fasts a day and breaks two days? He said: I wish I were given strength for that. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted in these successive reports fasting a day and breaking a day throughout all time, this indicates that fasting what is after the middle of Sha'ban is included in the permission the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gave to Abdullah ibn Amr. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3151","Chapter 10 - Kissing for the fasting one. 3357 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Jubayr, from Abu Yazid al-Dinni, from Maymunah bint Sa'd, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about kissing for the fasting one, and he said: They both broke their fast. Abu Ja'far said: A group adopted this and said: A man may not kiss during his fast, and if he kisses he has broken his fast. They also argued for that by what:"
"3152","3358 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, he said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Umar ibn Hamzah narrate to you? He said: Salim informed me, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Umar said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a dream, and I saw him not looking at me, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the matter with me? He said: Are you not the one who kisses while fasting? I said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I shall not kiss after this while fasting; he acknowledged it, then said: Yes. They also argued for that by what is reported from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him."
"3153","3359 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Hani, who was called al-Hazhiz, he said: Abdullah was asked about kissing for the fasting one, and he said: He makes up another day."
"3154","3360 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal, from al-Hazhiz, from Abdullah, the like of it. They also argued for that by what is reported from Umar, of his saying:"
"3155","3361 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar used to forbid kissing for the fasting one."
"3156","3362 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Imran ibn Muslim, from Zadham, he said: Umar said: To bite upon a coal is dearer to me than to kiss while fasting. They also argued for that by what is reported from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib."
"3157","3363 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib about a man kissing his wife while fasting, and he said: His fast is broken. Others disagreed with them and saw nothing wrong with kissing for the fasting one if it is not feared it will lead him to other than it, of what the fasting one is prohibited from. Part of their proof against what the people of the first view argued against them was that there is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), permitting kissing for the fasting one, what is more manifest than the hadith of Maymunah bint Sa'd and more deserving to be taken. It is what:"
"3158","3364 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Abd al-Malik ibn Sa'id al-Ansari, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: I felt desire one day and kissed while fasting, then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I did a grave matter today; I kissed while fasting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What do you think if you rinsed with water while fasting? I said: There is nothing wrong with that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then in what?"
"3159","3365 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shabab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Layth ibn Sa'd informed us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. This hadith is sound in chain, well-known to the narrators, unlike the hadith of Maymunah bint Sa'd which Abu Yazid al-Dabbi reported from her, a man unknown; the hadith of those we mentioned should not be opposed by the hadith of his like. Moreover, his hadith may bear a meaning other than the meaning of this hadith of Umar: the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer therein may be an answer to a question asked about two specific fasting persons, given their weak control of themselves, so he said that about them both, meaning that if kissing came from them both, other than it of what may harm them accompanied it. This is more fitting than what its meaning was carried upon, so that it does not contradict others. As for the hadith of Umar ibn Hamzah, its chain is not like the hadith of Bukayr we mentioned, for Umar ibn Hamzah is not like Bukayr ibn Abdullah in eminence, standing in knowledge and precision. Even if the two were equal, the hadith of Bukayr would be more deserving, for it is a saying from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while awake, by which proof is established against Umar, while the hadith of Umar ibn Hamzah is only a saying he related from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in sleep, by which proof is not established; what establishes proof is more deserving than what does not. Then this Ibn Umar narrated from his father what Umar ibn Hamzah related in his hadith, then said after his father contrary to that."
"3160","3366 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Mawraq, from Ibn Umar, that he was asked about kissing for the fasting one, and he permitted it for the old man and disliked it for the young man. This indicates that this, in his view, was more deserving than what Umar narrated to him of what Umar ibn Hamzah mentioned in his hadith. As for what they argued of the saying of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, contrary to that is also reported from him."
"3161","3367 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Tariq, from Hakim ibn Jabir, he said: Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to touch his wife while fasting. This hadith and what al-Hazhiz reported from Abdullah counterbalance each other. As for what they mentioned of the saying of Sa'id - meaning Ibn al-Musayyib - that his fast is broken, what is reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), likening that to rinsing the mouth, is more deserving than the saying of Sa'id. Then a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held that, which we shall mention from them at the end of this chapter, if Allah wills. Successive reports came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to kiss while fasting. Among that is what:"
"3162","3368 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would take of kisses while fasting."
"3163","3369 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Arubah, from Ayyub, he said: Abdullah ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. I did not know what it was until it was said: Kissing."
"3164","3370 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Al-Wahbi - he is Ahmad ibn Khalid - narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss her while fasting."
"3165","3371 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3166","3372 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Abdullah, from Abu Bakr ibn al-Munkadir, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Zaynab bint Abi Salamah, from Umm Salamah, that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kissed me while fasting."
"3167","3373 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Talhah ibn Yahya informed us, from Abdullah ibn Farrukh, he said: A woman came to Umm Salamah, and said: My husband kisses me while I am fasting. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss me while he was fasting and I was fasting."
"3168","3374 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Shutayr ibn Shakl, from Hafsah bint Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he kissed while fasting."
"3169","3375 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Muslim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3170","3376 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, he said: My father narrated to me that Ali ibn al-Husayn informed him, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss her while fasting."
"3171","3377 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3172","3378 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Isma'il al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3173","3379 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Sa'id informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"3174","3380 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3175","3381 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Al-Qasim narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it. He added: She used to say: Which of you controls his desire as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did?"
"3176","3382 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I said to Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim: Did your father narrate to you, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss her while fasting? He lowered his head, felt shy for a little and was silent, then said: Yes."
"3177","3383 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah - he is Ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi - narrated to us, he said: Al-Walid - he is Ibn Muslim - narrated to us, he said: Al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Yahya, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me: From Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss her while fasting."
"3178","3384 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr - he is Ibn Bakr - narrated to us, he said: Al-Awza'i narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3179","3385 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Abu Salamah informed me that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said, and he mentioned the like of it."
"3180","3386 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Nafi', from Abdullah ibn Umar, he said: My father gathered my family for me in Ramadan and brought them to me, then I entered upon Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and asked her about kissing - meaning - for the fasting one, and she said: There is nothing wrong with that; one who was the best of people used to kiss."
"3181","3387 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, from al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss while fasting."
"3182","3388 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Talhah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Ma'mar, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wanted to kiss me, and I said: I am fasting. He said: And I am fasting, then he kissed me."
"3183","3389 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not refrain from our faces while fasting."
"3184","3390 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I and Abdullah ibn Mas'ud went to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, to ask her about touching, then we left without asking her. We returned and said: O Mother of the Believers, did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) touch while fasting? She said: Yes, and he was the most controlling of his desire among you. The questioning of Abdullah of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, about this is proof that he had nothing about that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him of it from him. This indicates that what is reported from him agreeing with that was later than what is reported from him contradicting that."
"3185","3391 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Awn, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad and Masruq, both said: We asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) touch while fasting? She said: Yes, but he controlled his desire more than you both, or his affair. The doubt is from Abu Asim."
"3186","3392 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' narrated to us, from Hurayth ibn Amr, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would sometimes kiss me and touch me while fasting; as for you, there is nothing wrong with it for the old, weak Shaykh."
"3187","3393 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Shayban Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from Amr ibn Maymun - he is al-Awdi - he said: We asked Aishah about a man kissing while fasting, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss while fasting."
"3188","3394 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Ziyad, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss me while I was fasting."
"3189","3395 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ulayy narrated to us, he said: I heard my father saying: Abu Qays, freedman of Amr ibn al-As, narrated to me, he said: Abdullah ibn Amr sent me to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Ask her: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss while fasting? If she says: No, then say: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informs people that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss while fasting. I came to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, conveyed the greeting from Abdullah ibn Amr, and said: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) kiss while fasting? She said: No. I said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informs people that he used to kiss while fasting. She said: Perhaps he could not restrain himself from her out of love; as for me, no. These reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to kiss while fasting are successive, indicating that kissing does not break the fast of the fasting one. If one says: That was of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was singled out with - do you not see the saying of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: Which of you controlled his desire as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did? It is said to him: This saying of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, is only that she does not trust them, nor do they trust themselves, with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) trusted himself with, for he was protected. Proof that kissing, in her view, does not break the fast of the fasting one is what we reported from her that she said: As for you, there is nothing wrong with it for the old, weak Shaykh. She meant by that that his desire is not feared. This indicates that whoever does not fear from kissing while fasting anything further, and is safe upon himself, it is permissible for him. We mentioned from her in some of these reports that she was asked about kissing for the fasting one, and she answered that question: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss while fasting. Had the ruling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, in her view, been other than the ruling of other people, what she knew of the act of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have been an answer to what she was asked about the act of others. Abdullah ibn Umar asked her, when his father gathered his family for him in Ramadan, about the like of that, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that. This, in our view, is because she trusted him. What we mentioned indicates the equality of the ruling of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and all people, in her view, regarding kissing when it is not accompanied by fear of what follows it of what it leads to. It is likewise so by reasoning, for we see intercourse, food and drink were all forbidden for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his fast as they are forbidden for all his community in their fast; then this kissing was lawful for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his fast, so by the reasoning of what we mentioned, it is likewise lawful for all his community in their fast too, and his ruling and theirs are equal in it, as they are equal in all we mentioned. There is also reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates the equality of his ruling and his community's ruling in that, which is what:"
"3190","3396 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, that a man kissed his wife while fasting and felt severe distress from that, so he sent his wife to ask for him about that. She entered upon Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and mentioned that to her, and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed her that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to kiss while fasting. She returned and informed her husband of that, which increased him in distress, and he said: We are not like the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Allah, Mighty and Majestic, permits for His Messenger what He wills. Then the woman returned to Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with her. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What is the matter with this woman? Umm Salamah informed him, and he said: Did you not inform her that I do that? Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I did inform her; she went to her husband and informed him, which increased him in distress, and he said: Allah permits for His Messenger what He wills. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grew angry and said: I am the most God-fearing of you toward Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and the most knowledgeable of you of His limits. This indicates what we mentioned. This is the explanation of this chapter by way of reports, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3191","3397 - And there has been narrated from the early predecessors concerning that what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from Salim al-Dawsi, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas - and a man asked him: Do you touch your wife while you are fasting? - and he said: Yes."
"3192","3398 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata ibn Yasar, that 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas was asked about kissing for the fasting person, and he permitted it for the old man and disliked it for the young man."
"3193","3399 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Nadr, that 'A'ishah bint Talhah informed him that she was with 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when her husband 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr came in to her while he was fasting, so 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said to him: What prevents you from drawing near to your wife and kissing her? He said: Should I kiss her while I am fasting? So 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said to him: Yes."
"3194","3400 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Ashajj, from Abu Murrah, the freedman of 'Aqil, from Hakim ibn 'Iqal, that he said: I asked 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: What is forbidden to me of my wife while I am fasting? She said: Her private part. So this is 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, stating what we have mentioned regarding what is forbidden for the fasting person of his wife and what is lawful for him of her; that indicates that kissing was permissible in her view for the fasting person who is safe from himself, and disliked for others, not because it is unlawful for him, but because he is not safe, if he does it, from being overcome by his desire until he falls into what is forbidden to him."
"3195","3401 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: 'Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Tha'labah ibn Su'ayr al-'Udhri - this is what Ibn Abi Maryam said, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had wiped his face - that he informed him that he heard the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding the fasting person from kissing, saying: It leads to what is greater than it. So this hadith has explained the reason for which those who disliked it disliked it for the fasting person: it is only their fear for him from it, that it may lead him to what is greater than it. That is proof that when that reason for which they forbade him from it is absent, it is permissible for him."
"3196","3402 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Isma'il al-Dimashqi al-'Attar narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from his father, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, asked 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, about the kiss of the fasting person, so 'Ali said: He should fear Allah and not return to it. So 'Umar said: This is close to that. The statement of 'Ali, 'and not return', may mean 'and not return to it a second time', i.e. because it is disliked for him due to his fast; or it may mean 'and not return', i.e. kissing time after time, so that it becomes frequent from him and his desire is stirred, and it is feared for him from that that he may commit what Allah has forbidden him. And the statement of 'Umar, 'this is close to that', means that what I disliked for him is close to what I permitted him, or that what I permitted him is close to what I disliked for him. So there is no proof in this hadith; rather, the proofs are in what preceded it of what we have mentioned before it."
"3197","11 - Chapter: The fasting person who vomits. 3403 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Awza'i, from Ya'ish ibn al-Walid ibn Hisham, from his father, from Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah, from Abu al-Darda, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomited and broke his fast. He said: So I met Thawban in the mosque of Damascus and he said: He spoke the truth; I poured his ablution water for him."
"3198","3404 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr al-Awza'i, from Ya'ish ibn al-Walid ibn Hisham, from Ma'dan ibn Talhah, from Abu al-Darda, then he mentioned the like of it. Ibn Abi Dawud said: Abu Ma'mar said: This is what 'Abd al-Warith said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr."
"3199","3405 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Judi narrated to us, from Balj, a man from Mahrah, from Abu Shaybah al-Mahri, he said: I said to Thawban: Narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vomit and break his fast. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that when the fasting person vomits he has broken his fast, and they argued for that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them, saying: If he deliberately vomits he breaks his fast, but if vomiting overcomes him he does not break it. And they said: It is possible that his statement, 'he vomited and broke his fast', means he vomited and became weak and so broke his fast, and this is possible in the language. The first group also argued for their view with what follows."
"3200","3406 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, he said: Abu Marzuq informed me, from Hanash, from Fadalah ibn 'Ubayd, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for a drink, and one of us said to him: Did you not wake up fasting, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes, but I vomited."
"3201","3407 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us. (H) 3408 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us. (H) 3409 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu Marzuq, from Hanash, from Fadalah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. It was said to them: And this too is like the first; it is possible that 'but I vomited' means: so I became too weak to fast and so broke my fast. There is no proof in these two hadiths that vomiting broke his fast; in them there is only that he vomited, then broke his fast after that. And there has been narrated, concerning the ruling of the fasting person when he vomits or deliberately vomits, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in explicit form what follows."
"3202","3410 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is overcome by vomiting while fasting, there is no makeup upon him, but whoever deliberately vomits, let him make it up. So this hadith explains the ruling of the fasting person when vomiting overcomes him or he deliberately vomits. The most proper course for us is to interpret the reports in a way that brings them into agreement and correctness, not in a way that sets them in conflict and contradiction. So the meaning of the first two hadiths is as we have described, so that their meaning does not contradict the meaning of this hadith. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reconciling the meanings of the reports. As for its ruling by way of reasoning: we see vomiting as a ritual impurity (hadath) according to some people, and not a hadath according to others; we see the emission of blood likewise; and all agree that when a fasting person bleeds a vein he does not thereby break his fast, and likewise if he had an ailment that burst open with blood from a place on his body - so the emission of blood from his body as we mentioned, and his extracting it, are the same in what we mentioned, and likewise they are the same regarding purification. And the emission of vomit without its producer extracting it does not invalidate the fast, so reason, upon what we mentioned, dictates that its emission by its producer extracting it likewise does not invalidate the fast. Since vomiting does not break his fast by reasoning, what overcomes him of vomit is even more so. This is the ruling of this chapter also by way of reasoning. However, following what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is more proper. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, and the generality of the scholars. And that has been narrated from a group of the early predecessors."
"3203","3411 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Malik and Sakhr ibn Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he said: Whoever deliberately vomits while fasting, makeup is upon him, and whoever is overcome by vomiting, there is no makeup upon him."
"3204","3412 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it."
"3205","3413 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad - meaning Ibn Salamah - narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, the like of it."
"3206","3414 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, the like of it."
"3207","3415 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hibban al-Sulami, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, the like of it."
"3208","12 - Chapter: The fasting person who is cupped. 3416 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from Bakr ibn 'Abdullah al-Muzani, from Abu Rafi', he said: I entered upon Abu Musa while he was being cupped at night, and I said: If only this had been in the daytime. He said: Are you commanding me to spill my blood while I am fasting? And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"3209","3417 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"3210","3418 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, they said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: A group of the people of Basrah bore witness in my presence, among them al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan, on the authority of Ma'qil al-Ashja'i, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was being cupped on the eighteenth night past of Ramadan, and he said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"3211","3419 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm al-Ash'ari, from Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"3212","3420 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3213","3421 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah al-Bablutti narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Qilabah narrated to me, he said: Abu Asma al-Rahabi narrated to me, from Thawban, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out in Ramadan on the eighteenth, passed by a man being cupped, and said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"3214","3422 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, he said: Abu Qilabah narrated to me that Abu Asma narrated to him that Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), narrated to him, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3215","3423 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Layth, from 'Ata, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"3216","3424 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Khalid and Mansur, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, from Shaddad ibn Aws, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed in Ramadan by a man being cupped, and said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast."
"3217","3425 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Abu Qilabah, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3218","3426 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi', he said: Dawud ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-'Attar narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The cupper and the one who seeks cupping have broken their fast."
"3219","3427 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that cupping breaks the fast of the fasting person, whether cupper or cupped, and they argued for that with these reports. Others disagreed with them, saying: Cupping does not break the fast of cupper or cupped. And they said: There is nothing in what you narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his statement, 'The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast', to indicate that that breaking of fast was on account of cupping. It is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reported that the two had broken their fast for another reason, describing them by what they were doing when he reported that about them - just as one says, 'The standing man is corrupt', not meaning he is corrupt by his standing, but that he is corrupt for a reason other than standing. And there has been narrated from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani - and he is one of those who narrated that hadith - concerning this meaning."
"3220","3428 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Rabi'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only said, 'The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast', because the two were backbiting. This meaning is a sound meaning; their breaking of fast is not like breaking fast by eating, drinking, and intercourse, but rather their reward was nullified by their backbiting, so by that they became two who broke fast - not a breaking of fast that obligates makeup upon them. This is as it is said: Lying breaks the fast of the fasting person - by it is not meant the breaking of fast that obligates makeup; it is only upon the nullification of reward by that, just as it is nullified by eating and drinking. This is parallel to the interpretation we adopted of what we mentioned, and a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated concerning that another meaning."
"3221","3429 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: We only disliked - or: cupping was disliked - for the fasting person on account of weakness."
"3222","3430 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Humayd, he said: Thabit al-Bunani asked Anas ibn Malik: Did you dislike cupping for the fasting person? He said: No, except on account of weakness."
"3223","3431 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil informed us, he said: Anas ibn Malik was asked about cupping for the fasting person, and he said: I did not consider cupping disliked for the fasting person except on account of hardship."
"3224","3432 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hadiyyah ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We would not abandon cupping except fearing hardship."
"3225","3433 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Jabir, from Abu Ja'far and Salim, from Sa'id and Mughirah, from Ibrahim and Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Cupping was only disliked for the fasting person fearing weakness. So these reports indicate that what cupping is disliked for in fasting is the weakness that befalls the fasting person, so that he breaks his fast on account of it by eating and drinking. And something like this meaning has been narrated from Abu al-'Aliyah."
"3226","3434 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: 'Asim al-Ahwal informed us that Abu al-'Aliyah said: It was only disliked fearing that he may faint. He said: So I informed Abu Qilabah of that, and he said to me: If he faints, he is given water to drink. And this meaning itself has also been narrated from Salim ibn 'Abdullah."
"3227","3435 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, he said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad mentioning the saying of the people, 'The cupper and the one cupped have broken their fast'. So al-Qasim said: If a man cupped his hand or part of his body, that would not break his fast. So Salim said: Cupping was only disliked for the fasting person fearing that he may faint and so break his fast. The meaning narrated in the interpretation of that from Abu al-Ash'ath is as though more fitting; for if weakness were what was intended by the prohibition, the cupper would not be included in that. Since the cupper and the cupped have been joined in that, it is more fitting that it be for one reason in which they are alike, like backbiting, in which they are alike, as Abu al-Ash'ath said. And there has also been narrated from al-Sha'bi and Ibrahim that they said: It was only disliked on account of weakness too."
"3228","3436 - Yazid - he is Ibn Sinan - narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: I asked Ibrahim about cupping for the fasting person, and he said: It was only disliked on account of weakness."
"3229","3437 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Dawud informed us, from al-Sha'bi, that al-Husayn ibn 'Ali was cupped while fasting. And al-Sha'bi said: Cupping was only disliked because it weakens him. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the permissibility of cupping for the fasting person what follows."
"3230","3438 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Ayyub, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while he was fasting."
"3231","3439 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad - and he is al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi - narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3232","3440 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me, from al-Hasan ibn Zayd, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3233","3441 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while he was in ihram, fasting."
"3234","3442 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Mas'ud ibn Sa'd al-Ju'fi narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped between Makkah and Madinah while he was fasting, in ihram."
"3235","3443 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. (H) 3444 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim and Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3236","3445 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was cupped while he was fasting."
"3237","3446 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, adding: while he was fasting, in ihram."
"3238","3447 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Imran narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was cupped while he was fasting, in ihram, between Makkah and Madinah."
"3239","3448 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Malik narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that Abu Taybah cupped the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was fasting, and he gave him his wage; had it been unlawful he would not have given it to him. So this act of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicates that cupping does not break the fast of the fasting person; had it been of what breaks the fast of the fasting person, he would not have been cupped while fasting. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reconciling the reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: we see the emission of blood, at its gravest, as being a hadath by which purification is broken, and we see excrement and urine, whose emission is a hadath by which purification is broken, yet does not break the fast. Reason, upon that, dictates that blood is likewise. And we see the fasting person is not broken by bleeding a vein, so cupping in reasoning is likewise. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3240","3449 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, that Salim ibn 'Abdullah and al-Qasim ibn Muhammad saw no harm in cupping for the fasting person, and they said: Tell me, if he were cupped on the back of his palm, would that break his fast?"
"3241","13 - Chapter: A man who wakes in a day of the month of Ramadan junub - does he fast or not? 3450 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Sumayy, the freedman of Abu Bakr, that he heard Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman saying: I and my father were with Marwan ibn al-Hakam, who was the governor of Madinah, when it was mentioned that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: Whoever wakes junub breaks his fast that day. So Marwan said: I adjure you to go to the two Mothers of the Believers, 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, and ask them about that. He said: So 'Abd al-Rahman went and I went with him until we entered upon 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and 'Abd al-Rahman greeted her, then said: O Mother of the Believers, we were with Marwan, and it was mentioned to him that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: Whoever wakes junub breaks his fast that day. So 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Evil is what Abu Hurayrah said. O 'Abd al-Rahman, do you turn away from what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do? He said: No, by Allah. She said: Then I bear witness upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to wake junub from intercourse, not a wet dream, then fast that day. He said: Then we went out until we entered upon Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, and he asked her about that, and she said as 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said. So we went out until we came to Marwan, and 'Abd al-Rahman mentioned to him what the two said. So Marwan said: I adjure you, O Abu Muhammad, to ride my mount, for it is at the door, and go to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, at his land in al-'Aqiq, and inform him of that. So 'Abd al-Rahman rode and I rode with him until we came to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and 'Abd al-Rahman spoke with him for a while, then mentioned that to him. So Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I have no knowledge of that; it was only an informant who informed me of it."
"3242","3451 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Awn narrated to us, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Ya'la ibn 'Uqbah, he said: I woke junub while I intended to fast, so I came to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him, and he said to me: Break your fast. So I came to Marwan, asked him, and informed him of the statement of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. So he sent 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and he asked her, and she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come out for the Fajr prayer with his head dripping from intercourse, then fast that day. So he returned to Marwan and informed him, and he said: Go to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and inform him. So he came to him and informed him, and he said: I did not hear it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); al-Fadl only narrated it to me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3243","3452 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn informed us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Ibn 'Awn said: So I said to Raja: Who narrated to you from Ya'la? He said: Ya'la narrated to me directly. Abu Ja'far said: So some went to what Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated of that from al-Fadl, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and held by it and followed it. Others disagreed with them, saying: He performs ghusl and fasts that day of his. They went in that to what we narrated in the first section from 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3244","3453 - And to what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Rawh narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrating, from his father, he said: I entered upon 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake junub, then perform ghusl, then go early to the mosque with his head dripping, then fast that day. So I informed Marwan of it, and he said: Go to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and inform him of that. So I said: He is a friend of mine, so excuse me. He said: I insist upon you to come to him. So I and my father set out to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and I informed him of that. So Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, knows better than me. Shu'bah said: And in the sahifah: More knowledgeable about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than me."
"3245","3454 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind informed us, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his brother Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that he used to fast and not break his fast. He entered upon his father one day while he was breaking his fast, and said to him: What is the matter with you today, breaking your fast? He said: Janabah befell me and I did not perform ghusl until morning, so Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, gave me fatwa to break my fast. So they sent to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, asking her, and she said: Janabah would befall the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would perform ghusl after morning, then go out with his head dripping water, lead his companions in prayer, then fast that day."
"3246","3455 - 'Ali narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah, from 'Abd Rabbihi, from Abu 'Iyad, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, that Marwan ibn al-Hakam sent him to Umm Salamah and 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with them both. He said: So I met her boy Nafi' - meaning Umm Salamah's, may Allah be pleased with her. He said: So I sent him to her, and he returned to me and informed me that she said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake junub from other than a wet dream, then wake fasting. Then he came to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and sent to her her boy Dhakwan Abu 'Amr, and she informed him that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake junub from other than a wet dream, then wake fasting. So I came to Marwan ibn al-Hakam and informed him of their statement. He said: I adjure you to come to Abu Hurayrah and inform him of their statement. So I came to him and informed him, and he said: They know better."
"3247","3456 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Sumayy, from Abu Bakr, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake junub, then fast that day."
"3248","3457 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go out to the Fajr prayer with his head dripping from the ghusl of janabah, then fast his day."
"3249","3458 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, the two wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be overtaken by Fajr while he was junub, then fast."
"3250","3459 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, the two wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that they both narrated to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"3251","3460 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it, adding: in Ramadan."
"3252","3461 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Sumayy, from Abu Bakr, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3253","3462 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3254","3463 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from 'Ata, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"3255","3464 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: 'Asim ibn Bahdalah informed us, from Abu Salih, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"3256","3465 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Uthman al-Qurashi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that."
"3257","3466 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata narrated to us, he said: Sa'id informed us, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from 'Amir ibn Abi Umayyah, from Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that also."
"3258","3467 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3259","3468 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3260","3469 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. (H) 3470 - And Yazid - he is Ibn Sinan - narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, adding: So Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, retracted his fatwa in light of this report. They said: Since the reports have successively established what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), it is not permitted for us to depart from that to something else. Part of the argument of the people of the first view against them in that was that they said: What Umm Salamah and 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated, they only reported it from the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while al-Fadl in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what contradicts that. So it is possible that the ruling of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that was upon what 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, mentioned in their hadith, while the ruling of the rest of people is upon what al-Fadl mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the two reports would not be contradictory, upon the method of reconciling the meanings of the reports. Part of the counter-argument for the others against them was that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, is the one who narrated the hadith of al-Fadl, yet he retracted his fatwa to the statement of 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with them both, and deemed that more proper than what al-Fadl narrated to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This is a proof in this chapter. Another proof: we have found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that the ruling of people in that is also like his ruling."
"3261","3471 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from 'Abdullah ibn Ma'mar al-Ansari, from Abu Yunus, the freedman of 'A'ishah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a man said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - while he was standing at the door and I was listening, O Messenger of Allah -: I wake junub while I intend to fast. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And I wake junub while I intend to fast, so I perform ghusl and fast. He said: O Messenger of Allah, you are not like us; Allah has forgiven you what preceded of your sin and what comes after. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry and said: By Allah, I hope indeed to be the most God-fearing of you toward Allah and the most knowledgeable of you of what I guard against. Since the answer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to that questioner was his informing about his own act in that, it is established by that that his ruling in that and the ruling of others is the same. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reconciling the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning in that: we see them unanimous that if a fasting person slept in the daytime and became junub, that does not take him out of his fast. So we wanted to consider: is he one who enters the fast while in that state, or is the ruling of janabah when it befalls the fast different from the ruling of the fast when it befalls it? We saw that the things which prevent entering the fast - menstruation and postpartum bleeding - when that befalls the fast or the fast befalls it, it is the same. Do you not see that a menstruating woman may not enter the fast while menstruating, and that had she entered the fast pure then menstruation befell her in that day, she is thereby out of the fast. So the things which prevent entering the fast are the things which, when they befall the fast, nullify it. And since janabah, when it befalls the fast, by their unanimous agreement does not nullify it, reason, upon what we mentioned, dictates that likewise when the fast befalls it, it does not prevent entering it. By that is established what agrees with what Umm Salamah and 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3262","14 - Chapter: A man who enters voluntary fasting then breaks it. 3472 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us. (H) 3473 - And 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us. (H) 3474 - And Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Harun ibn Umm Hani or Ibn bint Umm Hani, from Umm Hani, she said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was fasting, and he handed me the remainder of his drink and I drank. Then I said: O Messenger of Allah, I was fasting, but I disliked refusing your leftover. He said: If it is makeup of a day of Ramadan, then fast a day in its place; and if it is voluntary, then if you wish make it up, and if you wish do not make it up. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this, claiming that whoever enters a voluntary fast then breaks it thereafter, from an excuse or without excuse, there is no makeup upon him. They argued for that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them, saying: Upon him is makeup of a day in its place. Part of the argument for them against the people of the first view was that the hadith of Umm Hani was narrated - as they mentioned - by Hammad ibn Salamah, while others narrated it, who are not below him in precision, contrary to that."
"3263","3475 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us. (H) 3476 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ibn ibn Umm Hani, from his grandmother Umm Hani - he heard it from her - she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a drink on the day of the conquest of Makkah, and he handed me and I drank it while I was fasting. I disliked refusing the remainder of his leftover, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, I was fasting. He said to her: Are you making up anything? She said: No. He said: Then it does not harm you."
"3264","3477 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3265","3478 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from the man of the family of Ja'dah ibn Hubayrah, from his grandmother Umm Hani, she said: I and Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her, entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest of Makkah. I sat on his right, and he called for a drink and drank, then handed me and I drank while I was fasting. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I do not see myself but that I have sinned, or committed a violation: you offered to me while I was fasting and I disliked refusing you. He said: Were you making up a day of Ramadan? She said: No. He said: Then no harm."
"3266","3479 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us. (H) 3480 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak, from Ibn Umm Hani, from Umm Hani, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it - except that he said: Then it does not harm you. So what Qays, Abu 'Awanah, and Abu al-Ahwas narrated contradicts what Hammad ibn Salamah narrated; for Hammad said in his hadith: If it is makeup of the month of Ramadan, then fast a day in its place, and if it is voluntary, then if you wish make it up, and if you wish do not make it up - that being upon no makeup being obligatory upon her if it was voluntary. While the others said in their hadith: Are you making up anything of Ramadan? She said: No. He said: Then it does not harm you - i.e. you are not sinful in your breaking this voluntary fast - and there is nothing in that to negate that a makeup day in its place is upon her. So the hadith of Simak has become muddled. Then we considered whether there is narrated from others what indicates something of that."
"3267","3481 - For Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar al-'Umari narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I and Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, woke fasting voluntarily, and food was gifted to us and we broke our fast upon it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon us and we asked him, and he said: Make up a day in its place. In this is proof that the ruling of breaking a voluntary fast obligates makeup. Part of what the people of the first view argued for the corruption of this hadith was that its origin is not from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah; rather, its origin is halted at one below 'Urwah."
"3268","3482 - That is because Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, that 'A'ishah and Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with them both, woke fasting, then he mentioned the like of it. They said: So this is the origin of the hadith. They said: And al-Zuhri was asked about that: Did he hear it from 'Urwah? He said: No. And they mentioned what 3483 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn 'Uyaynah saying: Al-Zuhri was asked about the hadith of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: I and Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, woke fasting. It was said to him: Did 'Urwah narrate to you? He said: No."
"3269","3484 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I said to Ibn Shihab: Did 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrate to you, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Whoever breaks his voluntary fast, let him make it up? He said: I did not hear anything of that from 'Urwah; rather, I was narrated it during the caliphate of Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Malik."
"3270","3485 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, adding: But some people narrated to me during the caliphate of Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Malik, from some of those who used to ask 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: I and Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, woke fasting, then he mentioned the hadith, meaning the like of the hadith of Rabi' al-Jizi. So this hadith has been corrupted by what entered its isnad of what we mentioned. And there has been narrated concerning that from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, also from another route what follows."
"3271","3486 - Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Rabi' al-Jizi - except that he said: Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, hastened me in speech, and she was her father's daughter."
"3272","3487 - Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn 'Isa al-Misri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. Part of what the people of the first view argued for the corruption of this hadith also was that Hammad ibn Zayd narrated it, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, halted, with no mention of 'Amrah in it."
"3273","3488 - Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us of that, he said: Abu Bakrah al-Ramadi narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id with that - meaning: and he did not mention 'Amrah. So this is the origin of the hadith. And there has also been narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, concerning this, from another route, what follows."
"3274","3489 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya ibn Talhah, from his paternal aunt 'A'ishah bint Talhah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, we have hidden hays for you. He said: I had indeed intended to fast, but bring it near; I shall fast a day in place of that. Muhammad - he is Ibn Idris - said: I heard Sufyan throughout most of my sittings with him not mentioning in it: I shall fast a day in place of that. Then I presented the hadith to him a year before he died, and he allowed in it: I shall fast a day in place of that. In this hadith is mention of the obligatoriness of makeup, and in the hadith of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, is what agrees with that. There is nothing in the hadith of Umm Hani to contradict what we mentioned. The least state of the hadith of 'Urwah and 'Amrah from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, is that it be halted at one below them both, and a connected hadith has agreed with it - the hadith of 'A'ishah bint Talhah. So the view by that, from the hadith standpoint, is more proper than the contrary view. As for reasoning in that: we see things become obligatory upon servants by their obligating them upon themselves - prayer, charity, fasting, hajj, and 'umrah. Whoever obligated something of that upon himself, saying: Such-and-such is upon me for Allah, fulfilling that became obligatory upon him. And we see things which servants enter and thereby obligate upon themselves by their entering them - prayer, fasting, hajj, and what we mentioned. Whoever entered a hajj or 'umrah then wanted to nullify it and exit from it had no right to that; by his entering it he is in the ruling of one who said: A hajj is upon me for Allah, so fulfilling it is upon him. If one says: We only prevented him from exiting them because he cannot exit them except by completing them, while prayer and fasting are not so, for they may be nullified and one exits them by speech, food, drink, and intercourse - it is said to him: Although hajj and 'umrah are as you mentioned, we see you claim that whoever has intercourse in them, their makeup is upon him, and makeup enters upon him after his emission from them. So you have imposed upon him entering their makeup, willing or not, on account of his corrupting them. What he makes up is a substitute for what became obligatory upon him by his entering it, not by a prior obligation from himself. Had the cause of hajj and 'umrah binding him when he entered ihram for them, and of the invalidity of exiting them, been what you mentioned - the impossibility of abandoning them - and were it not for that he could exit them, just as he could exit prayer and fasting by what we mentioned of things that exit them, then makeup of them would not be obligatory upon him, since he is unable not to enter it. Since that does not remove the obligatoriness of makeup from him, and in that he is like one upon whom is makeup of a hajj he obligated upon himself for Allah by his tongue, likewise in reasoning is one who enters prayer or fasting: he thereby obligated it for Allah upon himself by his entering it, then exited it, so its makeup is upon him. It is also said to him: We see 'umrah of what may be abandoned after entering it, in our view and yours, and the Sunnah came from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that in his statement to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: Leave the 'umrah aside and enter ihram for hajj. We shall mention that with its isnad in its place in this book of ours, Allah willing. So one who enters 'umrah, though able to abandon it and exit it, may not exit it and nullify it, then have no makeup upon him. One who enters it without a prior obligation from himself for it may not exit it before completing it except from an excuse; if he exits it and nullifies it, with excuse or without, its makeup is upon him. Prayer and fasting are likewise in reasoning: one who enters them may not exit them and nullify them except from an excuse, and if he exits them before completing them, with excuse or without, their makeup is upon him. This is the reasoning in this chapter, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. The like of that has also been narrated from more than one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3275","3490 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Sa'id ibn Abi al-Hasan, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he informed his companions that he was fasting, then came out to them with his head dripping, and they said: Were you not fasting? He said: Yes, but a slave girl of mine passed by me and pleased me, so I had intercourse with her - she was beautiful, I desired her - and I shall make it up another day."
"3276","3491 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn al-Jassas narrated to me, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: I fasted the day of 'Arafah and fasting exhausted me, so I broke my fast. I asked 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar about that, and he said: Another day in its place."
"3277","15 - Chapter: Fasting the day of doubt. 3492 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Sa'id al-Ashajj narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid Sulayman ibn Hayyan al-Azdi al-Ahmar narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Qays, from Abu Ishaq, from Silah, he said: We were with 'Ammar when a roasted sheep was brought, and he said to the people: Eat. A man of the people withdrew and said: I am fasting. 'Ammar said: Whoever fasts the day that is doubted has disobeyed Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Ja'far said: So a group disliked fasting the day that is doubted, and argued for that with this hadith. Others disagreed with them, seeing no harm in fasting it voluntarily. They said: The disliked fast in this hadith is only fasting it as part of Ramadan; as voluntary, there is no harm in it. They argued for that with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) elsewhere than this place, of his statement: Do not precede Ramadan by a day or two days, unless it coincides with a fast one of you used to fast, then let him fast it."
"3278","6 - Book of the Rites of Hajj. 1 - Chapter: A woman who finds no mahram - is the obligation of hajj due upon her or not? 3493 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Amr - he heard Abu Ma'bad, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed the people and said: No woman shall travel except with a dhu mahram with her, and no man shall enter upon her except with a dhu mahram with her. A man stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have been enlisted in such-and-such expedition, and I wanted to perform hajj with my wife. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform hajj with your wife."
"3279","3494 - Yunus ibn 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from 'Amr, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3280","3495 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Abu Ma'bad, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3281","3496 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman shall not travel except with a dhu mahram with her. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to the view that a woman shall not travel a journey, near or far, except with a dhu mahram, and they argued for that with these reports. Others disagreed with them, saying: Every journey less than a barid, she may travel it without a mahram; every journey a barid or more, she may not travel it except with a mahram. They argued for that with what follows."
"3282","3497 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar - he is al-Darir - narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih informed us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman shall not travel a barid except with a husband or a mahram relative."
"3283","3498 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Suhayl, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. They said: In the timing by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the barid is proof that what is less than it is contrary to it. Others disagreed with them, saying: When the journey is less than a day, she may travel it without a mahram; every journey a day or more, she may not travel it except with a mahram. They argued for that with what follows."
"3284","3499 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Shayban ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Sa'id, from his father, that he heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman to travel a day or more except with a dhu hurmah with her."
"3285","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it - except that he did not say: or more."
"3286","3501 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3287","3502 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed us. (H) 3503 - And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. They said: In the timing by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of a day is proof that what is less than it is contrary to it. Others disagreed with them, saying: Every journey less than two nights, she may travel it without a mahram; every journey two nights or more, she may not travel it without a mahram."
"3288","3504 - And they argued for that with what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Qaza'ah, the freedman of Ziyad, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: A woman shall not travel a journey of two nights except with a husband or a dhu mahram."
"3289","3505 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. They said: In the timing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that of two nights is proof that the ruling of what is less than them is contrary to their ruling. Others disagreed with them, saying: Every journey three days or more, she may not travel it except with a mahram; every journey less than that, she may travel it without a mahram. They argued for that with what follows."
"3290","3506 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman to travel a journey of three days except with a mahram."
"3291","3507 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Karim ibn Malik narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3292","3508 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not lawful for a woman to travel a journey of three days except with a man whose marriage to her is forbidden."
"3293","3509 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Isa and 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman shall not travel a journey of three days or more except with her husband, her son, her brother, or a mahram relative of hers - except that Ibn Numayr said in his hadith: more than three."
"3294","3510 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it, saying: A journey of three days."
"3295","3511 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il Abu Salamah narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Suhayl narrated to us, from his father and from al-Maqburi - both narrated to him from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him - raising it, he said: A woman shall not travel more than three nights except with a husband or a mahram relative. They said: In the timing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the three in that is proof that the ruling of what is less than the three is contrary to that. Among those who held this view are Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. All these reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agree on the prohibition of a journey of three days for a woman without a dhu mahram, and differ on what is less than the three. So we considered that, and found the prohibition of a journey without a mahram of a distance of three days or more established by all these reports; his timing of three days in that was permission for travel less than the three for her without a mahram - for otherwise there would be no meaning in his mentioning the three. He prohibited an absolute prohibition, and did not speak superfluous speech; rather, he mentioned the three so that it be known that what is less than it is contrary to it. Thus does the wise one speak with what indicates other than it, to spare him mentioning what that speech of his indicates - and he does not speak speech that indicates nothing else when he can speak speech that indicates something else. This is a favor from Allah upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in granting him the comprehensive speech which others by nature lack the power for. Then we returned to our subject: since he mentioned the three, and by his mentioning it the permissibility of what is less than it was established, then what is narrated from him of preventing her from travel less than the three - the day, the two days, and the barid - each one of those reports, and the report narrated of the three, whenever one is after the one contradicting it, it abrogates it: if the prohibition of a day's journey without a mahram is after the prohibition of a three-day journey without a mahram, it abrogates it; and if the report of the three is the later, it abrogates it. So it is established that one of the meanings less than the three abrogates the three, or the three abrogates them. The report of the three is thus in one of two cases: either it is the earlier, or it is the later. If it is the earlier, it permitted travel of less than three without a mahram, then after it came the prohibition of travel of what is less than the three without a mahram, forbidding what the first hadith forbade and adding another prohibition - what is between it and the three. So the three must be applied upon what the report mentioned in it obligated. If it is the later and others the earlier, it abrogates what preceded it, and what preceded it need not be acted upon. So the hadith of the three must be applied in all cases, while what contradicts it must be applied if it is the later, and not if it is the earlier. What we must apply and take in both cases is more proper than what must be applied in one case and abandoned in another. In the establishment of what we mentioned is proof that a woman may not perform hajj, when between her and the hajj is a journey of three days, except with a mahram. When she lacks a mahram and between her and Makkah is the distance we mentioned, she is not one who finds the way by whose existence hajj becomes obligatory upon her. And a group said: There is no harm in a woman traveling without a mahram. They argued for that with what follows."
"3296","3512 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she heard her saying concerning a woman who performs hajj with no dhu mahram with her: Not all of them have a dhu mahram."
"3297","3513 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from al-Layth, that Ibn Shihab narrated to him, from 'Amrah, that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was informed that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri gives fatwa that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not proper for a woman to travel except with a mahram with her. So she said: Not all of them have a dhu mahram. The argument against them in that is what the successively-established reports we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) convey; they are a proof against all who oppose them. If one says: Hajj did not enter into the travel prohibited in those reports - the argument against that speaker is the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas with which we began mentioning in this chapter, in which he says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us and said: No woman shall travel except with a mahram. A man said to him: I wanted to perform hajj with my wife, though I have been enlisted in such-and-such expedition. He said: Perform hajj with your wife. That indicates she ought not perform hajj except with him; otherwise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have said to him: What need has she of you, since she goes out with the Muslims - so you proceed on your way in what you enlisted for. In the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refraining from commanding him that, and his commanding him to perform hajj with her, is proof that hajj is not proper for her except with him. If one says: You narrated from Ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A woman shall not travel a journey of three days except with a dhu mahram - yet there has been narrated from him, of his statement after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the contrary of that - he mentioned what follows."
"3298","3514 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, that Nafi' narrated to him that he used to travel with Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, freedwomen clients of his, with no dhu mahram with them. It is said to him: This is no contradiction of what we narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); for we did not narrate from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a prohibition of a woman traveling any journey whatsoever except with a mahram. Rather, we narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prohibited a woman traveling a journey of three days except with a dhu mahram - that prohibiting her the travel whose distance is the three except with a mahram, and permitting what is less in distance without a mahram. So the travel which those freedwomen traveled with him without a mahram may well be the travel which did not enter into what we narrated from him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prohibition. Others argued for the permissibility of travel for a woman without a mahram with what is narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she used to travel without a mahram. 3515 - One of our companions narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Muqatil al-Razi - I know it only from Hakkam al-Razi - he said: I asked Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him: May a woman travel without a mahram? He said: No; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited a woman traveling a journey of three days or more except with her husband, her father, or a relative of hers. Hakkam said: So I asked al-'Arzami, and he said: There is no harm in that. 'Ata narrated to me that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to travel without a mahram. He said: So I came to Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and informed him of that. So Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Al-'Arzami did not know what he narrated; the people were a mahram to 'A'ishah - with whichever of them she traveled, she traveled with a mahram - while the people are not so to women other than her. All that we established in this chapter - of preventing a woman from a journey of a distance of three days except with a mahram, of permitting what is less than that of travel for her without a mahram, and that the obligation of hajj is not due upon a woman except by her finding a mahram along with finding the rest of the way by whose existence the obligation of hajj becomes due - is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3299","2 - Chapter: The mawaqit which one intending ihram ought not pass. 3516 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed for the people of Madinah Dhu al-Hulayfah, for the people of al-Sham al-Juhfah, for the people of Najd Qarn, and for the people of Yemen Yalamlam - and I did not hear it from him. It was said to him: And al-'Iraq? He said: There was no 'Iraq that day."
"3300","3517 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn Yasar, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar, and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that the people of al-'Iraq have no fixed time for ihram like the fixed times of the other lands."
"3301","3518 - And they argued for that with this hadith, saying: Likewise the rest of the other hadiths narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning the mawaqit of ihram - in none of them is there mention of al-'Iraq. Then they mentioned in that what Yunus and Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid and Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed for the people of Madinah Dhu al-Hulayfah, for the people of al-Sham al-Juhfah, for the people of Najd Qarn, and for the people of Yemen Yalamlam, then said: They are for them, and for all who come upon them from others. Whoever's family is short of the miqat, then from wherever he wishes, until that comes upon the people of Makkah."
"3302","3519 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated to us, he said: I asked 'Amr ibn Dinar about a pilgrim woman who passed by Madinah and came to Dhu al-Hulayfah while menstruating, and I said: Would it suffice her if she went ahead to al-Juhfah and entered ihram from it? 'Amr said: Yes. Tawus narrated to us - and do not think among us anyone more truthful of tongue than Tawus - he said: Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed, then he mentioned the like of it - except that he did not mention from his statement, 'Whoever's family is...' to the end of the hadith. They said: Likewise the people of al-'Iraq: whatever of these mawaqit they come upon is a miqat for them, and what is other than them is no miqat for them."
"3303","3520 - And they mentioned in that also what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people of Madinah enter ihram from Dhu al-Hulayfah, the people of al-Sham from al-Juhfah, and the people of Najd from Qarn. 'Abdullah said: And it reached me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people of Yemen enter ihram from Yalamlam."
"3304","3521 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. (H) 3522 - And 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 3523 - And Sufyan said: From 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed for the people of Madinah Dhu al-Hulayfah, for the people of al-Sham al-Juhfah, for the people of Najd Qarn, and for the people of Yemen Yalamlam."
"3305","3524 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Others disagreed with them, saying: Rather, the miqat of the people of al-'Iraq is Dhat 'Irq; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed that for them, just as he timed the rest of the mawaqit for their people. They mentioned in that what follows."
"3306","3525 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Yazid al-Qatrubulli and Hisham ibn Bahram al-Mada'ini narrated to us, they said: al-Mu'afa ibn 'Imran narrated to us, from Aflah ibn Humayd, from al-Qasim, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed for the people of Madinah Dhu al-Hulayfah, for the people of al-Sham and Egypt al-Juhfah, for the people of al-'Iraq Dhat 'Irq, and for the people of Yemen Yalamlam."
"3307","3526 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: And Abu al-Zubayr informed me, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard him asked about the muhill, and he said: I heard - then he stopped; I think he means the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The people of Madinah enter ihram from Dhu al-Hulayfah, and the other road from al-Juhfah; the people of al-'Iraq enter ihram from Dhat 'Irq; the people of Najd enter ihram from Qarn; and the people of Yemen enter ihram from Yalamlam."
"3308","3527 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hafs - he is Ibn Ghiyath - narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from 'Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed for the people of Madinah Dhu al-Hulayfah, for the people of al-Sham al-Juhfah, for the people of Yemen Yalamlam, and for the people of al-'Iraq Dhat 'Irq."
"3309","3528 - Yahya ibn 'Uthman and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Suwayd informed me, he said: Hilal ibn Zayd narrated to me, he said: Anas ibn Malik informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timing for the people of Madinah Dhu al-Hulayfah, for the people of al-Sham al-Juhfah, for the people of Basrah Dhat 'Irq, and for the people of al-Mada'in al-'Aqiq, a place near Dhat 'Irq. So established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by these reports is the timing of the people of al-'Iraq, as established is the timing of others by the reports before them. And this 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his timing what we mentioned from him in the section before this."
"3310","3529 - Then 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated to us, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) timed for the people of Madinah Dhu al-Hulayfah, for the people of al-Sham al-Juhfah, for the people of Yemen Yalamlam, and for the people of Ta'if Qarn. Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: And the people said for the people of the east: Dhat 'Irq. So this Ibn 'Umar reports that the people said that, and by the people Ibn 'Umar means none but the people of proof and knowledge of the Sunnah. It is impossible they said that by their opinions, for this is not of what is said by opinion; rather, they said it by what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them of. One says: How can the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have timed for the people of al-'Iraq that day what he timed, when al-'Iraq only came after him? It is said to him: Just as he timed for the people of al-Sham what he timed, though al-Sham was only conquered after him. If by what he timed for the people of al-Sham he means those in the direction conquered then on the side of al-Sham, likewise by what he timed for the people of al-'Iraq he means those in the direction conquered then on the side of al-'Iraq, like the mountain of Tayy and its environs. And if what he timed for the people of al-Sham is only for what he knew by revelation - that al-Sham would become a land of Islam - likewise what he timed for the people of al-'Iraq is only for what he knew by revelation - that al-'Iraq would become a land of Islam. For he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned what the people of al-'Iraq would do regarding their zakahs, along with his mentioning what the people of al-Sham would do regarding their zakahs."
"3311","3530 - 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us. (H) 3531 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us. (H) 3532 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, they said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Al-'Iraq has withheld its qafiz and its dirham; al-Sham has withheld its mudy and its dinar; Egypt has withheld its irdabb and its dinar; and you have returned as you began, and you have returned as you began, and you have returned as you began. Then the flesh and blood of Abu Hurayrah bear witness to that - some of them adding over others in the story of the hadith. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned what the people of al-'Iraq would do of withholding zakah before there was an 'Iraq, and mentioned the like of that concerning the people of al-Sham and the people of Egypt, before al-Sham and Egypt existed, for what Allah informed him of their coming to be after him. Likewise what he mentioned of timing for the people of al-'Iraq, along with his mentioning timing for the others mentioned, is for what Allah informed him would be after him. What we mentioned of establishing these mawaqit we described for the people of al-'Iraq and those we mentioned with them is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"3312","3 - Chapter: From where should the ihlal be? 3533 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed at Dhu al-Hulayfah, then his mount was brought and he rode it. When it stood straight with him upon al-Bayda, he entered ihram."
"3313","3534 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in the Farewell Hajj, rode his she-camel al-Qaswa; when it stood straight with him upon al-Bayda, he entered ihram."
"3314","3535 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amr - he is al-Awza'i - narrated to us - from 'Ata - he is Ibn Abi Rabah - that he heard him narrating, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him - meaning he heard him informing about the ihlal of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Dhu al-Hulayfah when his mount stood straight with him. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this and preferred ihram from al-Bayda, for the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering ihram from it. Others disagreed with them, saying: It is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram from it not because he intended his ihram to be from it specifically for a merit in entering ihram from it over entering ihram from other than it. We see him doing things in his hajj in places not for a merit he intended in those places by which others of places are surpassed - among that, his stopping at al-Muhassab of Mina. That was not because it is Sunnah, but for another reason about which people differed, what it is. There has been narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in that what follows."
"3315","3536 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn 'Iyad informed us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said to him: It was only a stop where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped, because it was easier for departure. And 'Urwah would not do tahsib, nor Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3316","And it is narrated from Abu Rafi' that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only commanded me to pitch his tent for him, and did not command me a specific place, so I pitched it at al-Muhassab. 3537 - Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated that to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Rafi'."
"3317","And there is narrated from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, what 3538 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Shu'bah - meaning the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas - that Ibn 'Abbas said: Al-Muhassab was only because the Arabs used to fear one another and would seek pasture and go out together, so the people continued upon it."
"3318","3539 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Salih, the freedman of al-Taw'amah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it - except that he said: Tamim and Rabi'ah used to fear one another."
"3319","3540 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr, from 'Ata, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Al-Muhassab is nothing; it is only a stop where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stopped. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did tahsib though that tahsib was not because it is Sunnah, likewise it is possible that he entered ihram when he came upon al-Bayda not because that is Sunnah. And a group denied that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram from al-Bayda, saying: He only entered ihram from beside the mosque. They narrated that from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3320","3541 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Salim, from his father, that he said: This Bayda of yours about which you lie against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only entered ihram from beside the mosque, meaning the mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah."
"3321","3542 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Musa, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3322","3543 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Musa, and he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. They said: And that was only after he mounted his mount."
"3323","3544 - And they mentioned in that what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Kaysan informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram when his mount stood straight with him, standing."
"3324","3545 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he would enter ihram when his mount stood straight with him, standing. He said: And Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to do it."
"3325","3546 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir informed me, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spent the night at Dhu al-Hulayfah until morning. When he mounted his mount and it stood straight with him, he entered ihram."
"3326","3547 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to us, from Anas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. They said: And it ought to be after his she-camel rises with him. They mentioned in that what follows."
"3327","3548 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from 'Ubayd ibn Jurayj, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enter ihram until his mount rose with him."
"3328","3549 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his foot in the stirrup and his mount rose with him standing, he would enter ihram from Dhu al-Hulayfah. When they differed in that, we wanted to consider whence their disagreement came."
"3329","3550 - For Isma'il ibn Ishaq ibn Sahl al-Kufi narrated to us by dictation, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: It was said to Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: How did people differ about the ihlal of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? One group said: He entered ihram in his prayer place; one group said: When his mount stood straight with him; and one group said: When he ascended upon al-Bayda. He said: I shall inform you about that: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram in his prayer place, and a group witnessed him and reported that. When his mount stood straight with him, he entered ihram, and a group witnessed him who had not witnessed him the first time, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has entered ihram now, and reported that. When he ascended upon al-Bayda, he entered ihram, and a group witnessed him who had not witnessed him the first two times, and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has entered ihram now, and reported that. The ihlal of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only in his prayer place. So 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, explained the aspect from which their disagreement came, and that the ihlal of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by which he began the hajj and entered into it was in his prayer place. By this we take. A man intending ihram ought to pray two rak'ahs, then enter ihram after them, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been narrated from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad in that something of what is narrated from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3330","3551 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Habib ibn Abi Thabit informed me that he heard al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did all that: he entered ihram when his mount stood straight with him, and he entered ihram when he came to al-Bayda."
"3331","4 - Chapter: How is the talbiyah? 3552 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Aban ibn Taghlib, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from 'Abdullah, he said: The talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: Labbayk Allahumma labbayk, labbayk la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa al-ni'mata lak."
"3332","3553 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah, from Abu 'Atiyyah, he said: 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I indeed remember how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite talbiyah. And she mentioned that also."
"3333","3554 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the talbiyah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was likewise, adding: wa al-mulk, la sharika lak."
"3334","3555 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Ayyub and 'Ubaydullah informed me, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it."
"3335","3556 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il al-Madini narrated to us, he said: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited talbiyah in his hajj likewise also."
"3336","3557 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ziyad ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Sharqi ibn Qutami narrated to us, he said: Abu Talq al-Aidhi informed me, he said: I heard Sharahil ibn al-Qaqa say: I heard Amr ibn Madikarib say: We were, not long ago, when we performed Hajj, saying (the pre-Islamic talbiya), and today we say as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us. He said: I said: And how did he teach you? Then he mentioned the talbiya like what is in the hadith before this. So the Muslims all agreed that this is how talbiya is made for Hajj. Except that some people said: There is no harm for a man to add therein whatever remembrance of Allah he wishes, and this is the saying of Muhammad (ibn al-Hasan), al-Thawri and al-Awzai."
"3337","And they argued for that with what 3558 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us. (H). 3559 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, both saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, Ibn Wahb said: Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl narrated to him. And Abu Amir said, from Abd Allah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he used to say: Among the talbiya of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was: Labbayka Ilaha al-Haqq, Labbayk (Here I am, God of Truth, here I am)."
"3338","And they also mentioned concerning that, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, 3560 - what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, that Malik informed him. (H). 3561 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ayyub and Ubayd Allah informed me, all saying: from Nafi who said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to add to the talbiya, over the talbiya which we already mentioned from him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Labbayk, Labbayk, Labbayka wa Sadayk, and all good is in Your hands, Labbayk, and desire and deeds are to You. They said: So there is no harm in adding in the talbiya the like of this and its similitude. Others opposed them in that, and said: One should not add to the talbiya beyond what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught the people, as we mentioned in the hadith of Amr ibn Madikarib, then he did it himself in the other hadith, and he did not teach the one he taught anything deficient of the talbiya, nor did he say to him: Say labbayk with whatever you wish, from what is of this kind; rather he taught him as he taught the takbir in prayer, and what should be done therein besides takbir. So just as one should not go beyond anything he taught in that, likewise one should not go beyond in the talbiya anything he taught. And something like this has been narrated from Sad."
"3339","3562 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Salamah, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, that he heard a man saying talbiya, saying: Labbayka Dha al-Maarij, Labbayk (Here I am, Lord of the Ascensions, here I am). Sad said: This is not how we used to say talbiya in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this Sad disapproved of addition over what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had taught them of the talbiya, so we adopt this."
"3340","Chapter 5: Perfuming oneself at ihram. 3563 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: I heard Qays ibn Sad narrating, from Ata, from Safwan ibn Yala ibn Umayyah, from his father, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Jiranah wearing a woolen jubbah, having dyed his beard and head yellow, and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have entered ihram and I am as you see. He said: Remove the jubbah from you, and wash off the yellow from you, and whatever you would do in your Hajj, do it in your Umrah. So some people adopted this hadith, and disapproved thereby perfuming at ihram, and they held what was narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab and Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3341","3564 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both, found the smell of perfume while at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and said: From whom is this pleasant smell? Muawiyah said: From me. Umar said: From you, by my life, from you, by my life. Muawiyah said: Do not be hasty against me, O Commander of the Believers; Umm Habibah, may Allah be pleased with her, perfumed me and made me swear. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: And I make you swear that you return to her and wash it off before her; so he returned to her and washed it, then caught up with the people on the road."
"3342","3565 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3343","3566 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Aslam, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it."
"3344","3567 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Nafi, from Aslam, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"3345","3568 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from his father, he said: I was with Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, at Dhu al-Hulayfah, and he saw a man wanting to enter ihram having oiled his head, so he ordered him and his head was washed with clay. Others opposed them in that, and saw no harm in perfuming at ihram. They said: As for the hadith of Yala, there is no proof in it for those who opposed us, because the perfume on that man was only yellow dye, which is khaluq, and that is disliked for a man not because of ihram, but because it is disliked in itself in the state of non-ihram and in the state of ihram; and what is permitted of perfume at ihram is only what is lawful in the state of non-ihram. And there has been narrated from Yala what clarifies that what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that man to wash off was khaluq."
"3346","3569 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Arubah narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from Ata, from Yala ibn Munabbih, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man who had said labbayk for Umrah wearing a jubbah and something of khaluq, so he ordered him to remove the jubbah and wipe off his khaluq, and to do in his Umrah what he does in his Hajj."
"3347","3570 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, that Ata ibn Abi Rabah narrated to him, from Ibn Yala ibn Munabbih, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3348","3571 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Ata narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Yala ibn Umayyah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar, except that he said: And wash off from you the trace of khaluq or yellow dye."
"3349","3572 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik and Mansur and Ibn Abi Layla informed me, from Ata, from Yala ibn Umayyah, that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have entered ihram with this jubbah of mine on me, and on his jubbah were spots of khaluq, and the people are laughing at me. He turned away from him for a while, then said: Take off this jubbah from you, and wash off this saffron from you, and do in your Umrah what you would do in your Hajj. So these reports clarified to us that that perfume which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to wash off was khaluq, and that is forbidden in the state of non-ihram and the state of ihram. So it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by ordering him to wash it off what was of his prohibition that a man dye himself with saffron, not because it was perfume he had applied before ihram which ihram then forbade him."
"3350","3573 - As for what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his forbidding men from dyeing with saffron: Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a man dye himself with saffron."
"3351","3574 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dyeing with saffron for men."
"3352","3575 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3353","3576 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ulayyah, he said: I think, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to dye himself with saffron."
"3354","3577 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dyeing with saffron."
"3355","3578 - Ibn Abi Imran and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both saying: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Suhayb, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade dyeing with saffron. Ali said - in what Ibn Abi Imran specifically mentioned -: Then I met Ismail and asked him about that, and informed him that Shubah had narrated it to us from him, and he said to me: This is not how I narrated it to him; I only narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade that a man dye himself with saffron. Ibn Abi Imran said: He meant by that that the prohibition which came from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that fell upon men specifically, not women."
"3356","3579 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ata ibn al-Saib, he said: I heard Abu Hafs ibn Amr narrating, from Yala, that he passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while dyed with khaluq, and he said: Do you have a wife? He said: No. He said: Go and wash it off."
"3357","3580 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us. (H). 3581 - And Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from a man of Thaqif, from Yala, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Thus Abu Bakrah said in his hadith. And Ali said in his hadith, from Ata ibn al-Saib, he said: I heard Abu Hafs ibn Amr, and Abu Amr ibn Hafs al-Thaqafi."
"3358","3582 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, from Qatadah - or Matar - from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Verily the perfume of men is scent without color, and verily the perfume of women is color without scent."
"3359","3583 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Ubayd narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"3360","3584 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Salm al-Alawi, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with yellow dye on him; when he stood up the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If only you would order this one to leave this yellow dye. He said: And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not confront a man with anything to his face."
"3361","3585 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Abu Jafar al-Razi narrated to us, from al-Rabi ibn Anas, from his two grandfathers, both saying: We heard Abu Musa say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of a man is not accepted while on his body is anything of khaluq."
"3362","3586 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, from Umm Habibah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the man who had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a need while dyed with khaluq, and he said: Go and wash. So I went and washed, then I came, and he said: Go and wash; so I went and took something and kept following its traces with it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in all these reports forbade men from dyeing with saffron. So he only ordered the man whom he ordered to wash off the perfume that was on him in the hadith of Yala because it was not of the perfume of men. And there is in that no evidence for the ruling of one who intends ihram, whether he may perfume himself with perfume that remains on him after ihram or not. As for what they narrated from Umar and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, in that, Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, opposed them in that."
"3363","3587 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Uyaynah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, that he said: I set out for Hajj and Uthman ibn Abi al-As accompanied me. When it was time for ihram he said: Wash your heads with this white khatmi, and let none of you touch anything besides it; so something entered my heart about that. I came to Makkah and asked Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them; as for Ibn Umar he said: I do not like it. As for Ibn Abbas, he said: As for me, I anoint my head with it thoroughly, then I like its remaining. So this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, opposed Umar, Uthman, Ibn Umar and Uthman ibn Abi al-As in that. And there has been narrated in that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates its permissibility."
"3364","3588 - Ibn Marzuq - meaning Ibrahim - narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: As if I am looking at the glistening of perfume in the parting of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he is in ihram."
"3365","3589 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, then he mentioned the like of it with his isnad."
"3366","3590 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, both saying: Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3367","3591 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hammad and Ata ibn al-Saib, from Ibrahim, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3368","3592 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3369","3593 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Israil informed me, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she used to perfume the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the best perfume she could find; she said: Until I would see the glistening of perfume on his head and beard."
"3370","3595 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu Zayd Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Umur narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri informed me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to perfume the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with fine ghaliyah at his ihram."
"3371","3596 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his brother Uthman ibn Urwah, from his father Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at his ihram with the best I could find."
"3372","3597 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with my hands for his ihram before he entered ihram."
"3373","3598 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me, that al-Qasim narrated to him, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his sacred state when he entered ihram. 3599 - Usamah ibn Zayd said: And Abu Bakr ibn Hazm narrated to me, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that."
"3374","3600 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3375","3601 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3376","3602 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Aflah - he is Ibn Humayd - narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3377","3603 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3378","3604 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his sacred state and for his non-sacred state."
"3379","3605 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: I asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: With what did you perfume the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: With the best perfume, at his becoming non-muhrim and before he entered ihram."
"3380","3606 - Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his sacred state and for his non-sacred state."
"3381","3607 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, he said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for non-ihram and ihram. Abu Jafar said: So these reports have successively come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitting perfume at ihram, and that it would remain in his partings after ihram. And that has also been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in what preceded of what we narrated in this chapter. And there has also been narrated in that from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3382","3608 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Tammam Abu al-Kurdus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Maymun ibn Yahya ibn Muslim ibn al-Ashajj narrated to me, from Makhramah ibn Bukayr, from his father, he said: I heard Usamah ibn Zayd say: I heard Aishah bint Sad say: I used to saturate the head of Sad ibn Abi Waqqas with perfume for his sacred state."
"3383","3609 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, he said: Dharrah narrated to me, she said: I used to coat the head of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, with musk and ambergris at her ihram."
"3384","3610 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us. (H). 3611 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Hukaymah - Abu Asim said: daughter of Abi Hakim - informed me, from her mother, daughter of al-Najjar, that the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to make headbands containing wars and saffron, and would bind with them the lower ends of their hair over their foreheads before entering ihram, then enter ihram; thus one of them adds over the other in the story of the hadith."
"3385","3612 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, that he used to perfume himself with fine ghaliyah at ihram. So this has come in that from those we mentioned in these reports among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreeing with what Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his perfuming at ihram. And this was the saying of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on them both. As for Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him, he held in that to what was narrated from Umar, Uthman ibn Affan, Uthman ibn Abi al-As and Ibn Umar of its dislike. And among the proof for him in that was that what was mentioned in the hadith of Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, of perfuming the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at ihram only contains that she used to perfume him when he wanted to enter ihram. So it is possible she would do this to him, then he would wash when he wanted ihram, so his washing would remove what was on his body of perfume, while its scent remains. If one says: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said in a hadith: I used to see the glistening of perfume in his partings after he entered ihram. It is said to him: It is possible that was after he had washed it as we mentioned, and likewise perfume: a man may wash it off his face or hand, so it goes while its glistening remains. Since what was narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, of that admits what we mentioned, we looked: is there in what was narrated from her anything indicating that?"
"3386","3613 - So Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn al-Muntashir, from his father, he said: I asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, about perfume at ihram, and he said: I do not like to wake up in ihram with the smell of perfume exuding from me. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, sent one of his sons to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, to let his father hear what she said. He said: Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I perfumed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he went around his wives and woke up in ihram, so Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, fell silent. Abu Jafar said: So this hadith indicates that there was washing between his ihram and her perfuming him, because he does not go around them except having washed. So as if she only intended by these hadiths to argue against one who disliked that the smell of perfume be found from the muhrim after his ihram, as Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, disliked that. As for the remaining of the perfume itself on the body of the muhrim after he entered ihram, even if he had perfumed himself with it before ihram, this hadith does not convey it, for its meaning is a subtle meaning. We have clarified the aspects of these reports, then we needed after that to know what is the aspect of the disagreement we are in, by way of reasoning. We considered that, and saw that ihram forbids wearing shirts, trousers, leather socks and turbans, and forbids perfume, and killing game and holding it. Then we saw that if a man wore a shirt or trousers before entering ihram, then entered ihram while it was on him, he is ordered to remove it, and if he does not remove it and leaves it on him, he is like one who wore it anew after ihram, so there is due upon him in that what is due upon him therein had he newly worn it after his ihram. And likewise had he hunted game while non-muhrim and held it in his hand, then entered ihram while it was in his hand, he is ordered to release it, and if he does not release it, his holding it after his ihram is like his holding it after his ihram of game that was from him after his ihram. Since what we mentioned is so, and perfume is forbidden for the muhrim after his ihram like the sacredness of these things, the remaining of perfume on him after his ihram, even if he had perfumed himself with it before his ihram, is like his perfuming himself with it after his ihram, by analogy and reasoning upon what we clarified. So this is the reasoning in this chapter, and we adopt it, and it is the saying of Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on him."
"3387","Chapter 6: What garments the muhrim wears. 3614 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid and Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us. (H). 3615 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, all saying: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Zayd say: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Arafah say: Whoever does not find a waist-wrapper, let him wear trousers, and whoever does not find sandals, let him wear leather socks."
"3388","3616 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. And he did not mention Arafah."
"3389","3617 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed me, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3390","3618 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was delivering a sermon, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3391","3619 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, then he mentioned the like of it. Except that he did not say: while he was delivering a sermon."
"3392","3620 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu al-Shatha, he said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed me, he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delivering a sermon, then he mentioned similar. I said: And he did not say: cutting them? He said: No."
"3393","3621 - al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Hibari al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever does not find sandals, let him wear leather socks, and whoever does not find a waist-wrapper, let him wear trousers. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted these reports and said: Whoever does not find a waist-wrapper while in ihram wears trousers, and nothing is upon him, and whoever does not find sandals wears leather socks, and nothing is upon him. Others opposed them in that, and said: As for what you mentioned of the muhrim wearing leather socks and trousers out of necessity, we say that, and we permit him to wear them for the necessity he is in. But we impose upon him - along with that - expiation, and there is not in what you narrated a negation of the obligation of expiation, nor is there in it nor in our saying any contradiction to anything of that. Because we did not say: He does not wear leather socks if he does not find sandals, nor trousers if he does not find a waist-wrapper. Had we said that we would be opposing this hadith, but we permitted him the clothing as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted him, then imposed upon him along with that expiation by the standing evidences imposing that. And his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whoever does not find sandals, let him wear leather socks, may also be taken to mean cutting them from below the ankles, so he wears them as he wears sandals. And his saying: Whoever does not find a waist-wrapper, let him wear trousers, may be taken to mean splitting the trousers, so he wears it as he wears the waist-wrapper. If this hadith was intended with this meaning, we do not oppose anything of that, and we say it and affirm it. The disagreement between us and you only occurred in interpretation, not in the hadith itself; because we directed the hadith to an aspect it admits, so recognize the place of disagreement of interpretation from the place of disagreement of hadith; for they are different, and do not impose upon one who opposes your interpretation opposition to that hadith. And Abd Allah ibn Umar has clarified from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) some of that."
"3394","3622 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Umar ibn Nafi, from his father, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What garments do we wear when we enter ihram? He said: Do not wear trousers, nor turbans, nor hooded cloaks, nor leather socks, except that one who has no sandals should wear leather socks below the ankles."
"3395","3623 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Said ibn Abi Arubah, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3396","3624 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3397","3625 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3398","3626 - Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan - he is Ibn Uyaynah - narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3399","3627 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3400","3628 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us. (H). 3629 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, both saying: from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it."
"3401","3630 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Dinar informed me, that he heard Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, say, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever does not find sandals, let him wear leather socks and cut them from at the ankles. So this Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informs from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the wearing of leather socks which he permitted for the muhrim, how it is, and that it is unlike what the non-muhrim wears. And Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, did not clarify in his hadith anything of that, so the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, is worthier of the two. And since what he permitted the muhrim of wearing leather socks is unlike what the non-muhrim wears, likewise what he permitted him of wearing trousers is unlike what the non-muhrim wears. So this is the ruling of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. As for reasoning upon that: we saw they did not differ about one who finds a waist-wrapper, that wearing trousers is not permitted for him, because ihram has forbidden him that. And likewise one who finds sandals, wearing leather socks is forbidden for him without necessity, so we wanted to look at wearing that by way of necessity, how it is, and whether it imposes expiation or not. We considered that and saw that ihram forbids things that were permitted before it, among them: wearing shirts, turbans, leather socks, trousers and hooded cloaks. And one who was compelled and found heat and covered his head, or found cold and wore his clothes, has done what is permitted for him to do, yet expiation is upon him along with that; and ihram also forbade him shaving the head except from necessity. And one who shaves his head from necessity has done what is permitted for him, while expiation is obligatory upon him. So shaving the head for the muhrim - in other than the state of necessity - when it is permitted in the state of necessity, its permission does not drop expiation; rather expiation in all that is obligatory in the state of necessity as it is in other than the state of necessity. And likewise wearing the shirt which was forbidden for him in other than the state of necessity: when there is necessity and that is permitted for him, the liability does not drop thereby, so expiation is obligatory upon him in all that. Necessity in anything we mentioned does not drop an expiation that would be due in something in other than the state of necessity; it only drops sins specifically. Likewise necessities in wearing leather socks and trousers do not necessitate dropping the expiations that would be due had those necessities not existed; rather they lift sins specifically. So this is the reasoning in this chapter as well, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"3402","Chapter 7: Wearing a garment touched by wars or saffron in ihram. 3631 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Abu Salih the scribe of al-Layth narrated to us, both saying: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wear a garment touched by wars or saffron. Meaning in ihram."
"3403","3632 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3404","3633 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"3405","3634 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted these reports, and said: Every garment touched by wars or saffron, it is not lawful to wear it in ihram even if washed, because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not clarify in these reports what was washed of that from what was not washed, so his prohibition is upon all that. Others opposed them in that and said: Whatever of that was washed until it no longer sheds, there is no harm in wearing it in ihram, because the dyed garment was only forbidden to wear in ihram since there had entered it of what is forbidden for the muhrim; so when it is washed and that exits from it, the meaning for which there was prohibition goes, and the garment returns to its first state before that which was washed from it befell it. They said: This is like the pure garment struck by impurity, so it becomes impure thereby, and prayer is not valid in it; then when it is washed until the impurity exits from it, it becomes pure, and prayer becomes valid in it. And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that that he excepted from what he forbade the muhrim of that, saying: Except that it be washed."
"3406","3635 - Fahd narrated to us about that, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us. (H). 3636 - And Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of the hadith we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, adding: Except that it be washed. Ibn Abi Imran said: And I saw Yahya ibn Main wondering at al-Himmani narrating this hadith, so Abd al-Rahman said to him: This is with me. Then he jumped up at once and brought his original, and produced from it this hadith from Abu Muawiyah, as Yahya al-Himmani mentioned it, so Yahya ibn Main wrote it from him. So it is established by what we mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) excepted washing from what had been touched by wars or saffron. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad - may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them - and that has been narrated from a number of the early ones."
"3407","3637 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that a man came to him and said to him: I want to enter ihram and I have only this garment, a garment dyed with saffron. He said: By Allah, do you find nothing else? So he swore, and he said: Wash it and enter ihram in it."
"3408","3638 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Layth, from Tawus, he said: If there is saffron or wars in the garment, and it is washed, there is no harm in entering ihram in it."
"3409","3639 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about a garment in which is wars or saffron, and it was washed: He saw no harm in entering ihram in it."
"3410","Chapter 8: A man enters ihram wearing a shirt, how should he take it off? 3640 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ata ibn Abi Labibah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Jabir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting in the mosque, and he tore his shirt from its opening until he took it out from his feet; the people looked at the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: I was ordered that my sacrificial camels which I sent be garlanded today, and marked on such-and-such, so I wore my shirt and forgot, so I would not take my shirt out from my head; and he had sent his sacrificial animals while staying in Madinah. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted this and said: The muhrim should not take it off as the non-muhrim takes off his shirt, because if he does that he covers his head, and that is forbidden for him, so he was ordered to tear it for that. Others opposed them in that and said: Rather he pulls it off, and they argued in that with the hadith of Yala ibn Umayyah who entered ihram wearing a jubbah, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who ordered him to pull it off. We already mentioned that in the chapter of perfuming at ihram. So that contradicts the hadith of Jabir which we mentioned, though its isnad is better than his isnad. If these things are established by soundness of isnad, then the hadith of Yala has with it of soundness of isnad what is not with the hadith of Jabir. As for the aspect of that by way of reasoning: we saw that those who disliked pulling off the shirt only disliked that because it covers his head when he pulls his shirt off. So we wanted to look: is covering the head in ihram forbidden in all ways or not? We saw the muhrim was forbidden from wearing caps, turbans and hooded cloaks, which is to clothe his head with something, as he was forbidden to clothe his body with the shirt. And we saw that had the muhrim carried something on his head, clothes or otherwise, there would be no harm in that, and that does not enter into what was forbidden of covering the head with caps and their like, because he is not wearing. So the prohibition was only upon covering that clothes the head, not upon other covering that is not clothing. And likewise bodies were forbidden to be clothed with the shirt, but not forbidden to be wrapped with waist-wrappers. Since what the prohibition fell upon of this in the head is only clothing, not mere covering that is not clothing, and when he pulls his shirt off and it touches his head, that is not clothing his head with anything, it is only covering his head. And it is established by what we mentioned that the prohibition of wearing caps did not fall upon covering the head, but only upon clothing the head in the state of ihram with what is worn in the state of non-ihram. So when what touches the head of the pulled-off shirt thereby exits the state of the forbidden head-covering, it is established that there is no harm in that, by analogy and reasoning upon what we mentioned. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And the early ones differed in that."
"3411","3641 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from al-Hasan."
"3412","3642 - And Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim and al-Shabi, that they said: If a man enters ihram wearing a shirt, let him tear it upon him until he exits from it."
"3413","3643 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Salim, from Said ibn Jubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"3414","3644 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, and Hammad, from Ibrahim, he said: If a man enters ihram wearing a shirt, one of them said: He tears it, and the other said: He takes it off from the side of his feet."
"3415","3645 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, that a man called Yala ibn Umayyah entered ihram wearing a jubbah, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to pull it off. Qatadah said: I said to Ata: We only thought he should tear it, so Ata said: Allah does not love corruption."
"3416","3646 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Maslamah al-Azdi, he said: I heard Ikrimah, and he was asked about a man who enters ihram wearing a qaba, he said: He takes it off. So this Ata and Ikrimah opposed Ibrahim, al-Shabi and Said ibn Jubayr, and adopted what we adopted of the hadith of Yala."
"3417","Chapter 9: What the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in ihram with in the Farewell Hajj. 3647 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj alone (ifrad)."
"3418","3648 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad - he is Ibn Musa - narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We set out seeing it as nothing but Hajj."
"3419","3649 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Hajj; among us were those who entered ihram for Umrah, among us those who entered ihram for Hajj and Umrah, and among us those who entered ihram for Hajj, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for Hajj. As for one who entered ihram for Umrah, he exited ihram, and as for one who entered ihram for Hajj, or combined Hajj and Umrah, he did not exit ihram until the Day of Sacrifice."
"3420","3650 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed me, he said: Alqamah ibn Abi Alqamah narrated to me, from his mother, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the people in the year of the Farewell Hajj, saying: Whoever wishes to begin with Umrah before Hajj, let him do so; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj alone."
"3421","3651 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother, from Asma, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived entering ihram for Hajj."
"3422","3652 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, in his long hadith, saying: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram with tawhid, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not add anything over the people, and we intended nothing but Hajj, and we did not know Umrah."
"3423","3653 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth and Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entering ihram for Hajj alone. Abu Jafar said: So some people adopted this, and said: Ifrad is better than tamattu and qiran, and they said: With it the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram in the Farewell Hajj. Others opposed them in that, and said: Tamattu with Umrah to Hajj is better than ifrad and qiran, and they said: It is what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in the Farewell Hajj. And they mentioned in that what:"
"3424","3654 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Ali and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, met at Usfan, while Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was forbidding mutah. Ali said to him: What do you want with a matter the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did, that you forbid it? He said: Leave us alone from you. He said: I cannot leave you. Then Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, entered ihram for both together."
"3425","3655 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, he said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, performed Hajj, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu? He said: Yes."
"3426","3656 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal ibn Abd al-Muttalib, that he narrated to him, that he heard Sad ibn Abi Waqqas and al-Dahhak ibn Qays, in the year Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan performed Hajj, while both were mentioning tamattu with Umrah to Hajj. Al-Dahhak said: None does that except one ignorant of the command of Allah. Sad said: Evil is what you said, O son of my brother. Al-Dahhak said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade that. Sad said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did it, so we did it with him."
"3427","3657 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it."
"3428","3658 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Ghunaym ibn Qays, he said: I asked Sad ibn Malik about mutah of Hajj, and he said: We did it while he was that day a polytheist at al-Urush, meaning Muawiyah, meaning the roofs of the houses of Makkah."
"3429","3659 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Muslim - he is al-Qurri - he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for Hajj, and he entered ihram for Umrah; whoever had a sacrificial animal with him did not exit ihram, and whoever had no sacrificial animal with him exited ihram; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Talhah were among those who had sacrificial animals with them, so they did not exit ihram."
"3430","3660 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mumin al-Marwazi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hamzah narrated to us, from Layth - he is Ibn Abi Sulaym. (H). 3661 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Layth, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu until he died, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, until he died, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, until he died, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, until he died. Sulayman said in his hadith: And the first who forbade it was Muawiyah."
"3431","3662 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Sharik, he said: I performed tamattu, then I asked Ibn Umar, Ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them, and they said: You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet; come, then circumambulate, then exit ihram."
"3432","3663 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad similar, except that he said: Abu Ghassan said: I think he said: To the Sunnah of your Prophet, do such, then enter ihram on the Day of Tarwiyah, and do such, and do such."
"3433","3664 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, he said: I performed tamattu and some people forbade me it, so I asked Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he ordered it for me, so I performed tamattu and slept, then one came to me in a dream and said: An accepted Umrah, and a righteous Hajj. So I came to Ibn Abbas and informed him, and he said: Allah is Greatest; the Sunnah of Abu al-Qasim, or the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3434","3665 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi - he is Ahmad ibn Khalid - narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, he said: I was sitting with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the mosque when a man from the people of Sham came to him and asked him about tamattu with Umrah to Hajj, so Ibn Umar said: Good and beautiful. He said: Your father used to forbid that. He said: Woe to you; if my father forbade that, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did it and ordered it, do you take by the saying of my father, or by the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: By the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Get up from me."
"3435","3666 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both saying: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, that Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu in the Farewell Hajj with Umrah to Hajj, and sacrificed and drove the sacrificial animal with him from Dhu al-Hulayfah; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began and entered ihram for Umrah, then entered ihram for Hajj, and the people performed tamattu with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Umrah to Hajj."
"3436","3667 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about his tamattu with Umrah to Hajj, and the tamattu of the people with him, like what Salim informed me of, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). If one says: You narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, at the beginning of this chapter the opposite of this. You narrated, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj alone. And you narrated from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: We set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Hajj; among us were those who entered ihram for Umrah, among us those who entered ihram for Hajj and Umrah, and among us those who entered ihram for Hajj, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for Hajj. And you narrated, from Umm Alqamah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of the Farewell Hajj performed Hajj alone and did not perform Umrah. It is said to him: It is possible the ifrad which this one mentioned is upon a meaning that does not contradict the meaning of what al-Zuhri narrated, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. That is because it is possible the ifrad which al-Qasim mentioned from Aishah, she only meant thereby ifrad of Hajj at the time he entered ihram, even if he had entered ihram after exiting from it with Umrah. So she meant that he did not mix it at the time of his ihram for it with ihram for Umrah, as others among those with him did. As for the hadith of Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: she informed that among them were those who entered ihram for Umrah without Hajj with it, and among them those who entered ihram for Hajj and Umrah, meaning joined together, and among them those who entered ihram for Hajj, and she did not mention in that tamattu. So it is possible those who had entered ihram for Umrah entered ihram after it for Hajj; her hadith does not negate anything of that; and she said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for Hajj alone. So it is possible that lone Hajj was after a preceding lone Umrah. So he had entered ihram for a lone Umrah upon what is in the hadith of al-Qasim, and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Urwah, then entered ihram after that for Hajj, upon what is in the hadith of al-Zuhri, from Urwah, until these reports agree and do not contradict. As for the meaning of what Umm Alqamah narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj alone and did not perform Umrah: it is possible she meant thereby that he did not perform Umrah at the time of his ihram for Hajj as some of those with him did, but he performed Umrah after that."
"3437","3668 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, that Abd Allah, the freedman of Asma bint Abi Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to him, that he heard Asma when she passed by al-Hajun say: May Allah bless the Messenger; we alighted with him here while our bags were light, our mounts few, our provisions little; so I performed Umrah, I and my sister Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and al-Zubayr, and so-and-so and so-and-so; then when we wiped the House we exited ihram, then we entered ihram in the evening for Hajj. So this Asma informs that whoever then began with Umrah entered ihram after it for Hajj, so became thereby mutamatti."
"3438","3669 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif, from Imran, he said: We performed tamattu with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the Quran was revealed about it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not forbid us, nor did anything abrogate it; then a man said by his opinion what he wished."
"3439","3670 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, he said: We performed tamattu in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the mutah of Hajj; he did not forbid us it, nor did Allah reveal about it a prohibition."
"3440","3671 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: We performed tamattu with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then when Umar took charge he addressed the people and said: Verily the Quran is the Quran, and verily the Messenger is the Messenger, and verily they were two mutahs in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): the mutah of Hajj - so separate between your Hajj and your Umrah, for it is more complete for your Hajj, and more complete for your Umrah; and the other, the mutah of women - so I forbid it and punish for it."
"3441","3672 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Two mutahs we did in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade us them, so we did not return to them. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying what indicates that it was likewise."
"3442","3673 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with them, that she said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What is the matter with the people, they exited ihram with Umrah, while you did not exit ihram from your Umrah? He said: I have matted my head, and garlanded my sacrificial animal, so I shall not exit ihram until I sacrifice. So this hadith indicates that he was mutamatti, because the garlanded sacrificial animal only prevents exiting ihram specifically in mutah. This is if that saying from him was after his tawaf for Umrah; and it is also possible that this saying was from him before he entered ihram for Hajj, and before he circumambulated for Umrah, so that was his ruling: were it not for his driving the sacrificial animal, he would exit ihram as the people exit ihram, after he circumambulates; then he did not circumambulate until he entered ihram for Hajj, so he became qarin. So the hadith of Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, which we mentioned, is not free of one of these two interpretations. Upon whichever it was in reality, it has negated the saying of one who said: He was mufrid with a Hajj preceded by no Umrah, nor was there Umrah with it. Others opposed them in that, and said: Rather qiran in that between Umrah and Hajj is better than ifrad of Hajj, and than tamattu with Umrah to Hajj. And they said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in the Farewell Hajj. And they mentioned in that what:"
"3443","3674 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr informed me, from al-Awzai, he said: Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah narrated to me, he said: Shaqiq ibn Salamah narrated to me, he said: A man from Taghlib - called Ibn Mabad - narrated to me, he said: I entered ihram for Hajj and Umrah together; when I came to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, I mentioned to him my ihram, and he said: You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet, or: to the Sunnah of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3444","3675 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed me, from Mansur and al-Amash, from Abu Wail, the like of it."
"3445","3676 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Mansur informed me, he said: I heard Abu Wail narrating, that al-Subayy, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3446","3677 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Kuhayl informed me, from Abu Wail, the like of it."
"3447","3678 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu Wail, the like of it."
"3448","3679 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Abu Wail, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3449","3680 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Abu Wail, the like of it."
"3450","3681 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Wail, he said: al-Subayy ibn Mabad said, then he mentioned similar. So those who denied qiran said: Umar's saying, may Allah be pleased with him: You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet, is only his supplication for him, not his approval of his deed. So among the proof against them in that, of what indicates that was not from Umar by way of supplication:"
"3451","3682 - That Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Shaqiq narrated to me, he said: al-Subayy ibn Mabad narrated to me, he said: I was newly quit of Christianity; when I embraced Islam I spared no effort to strive, so I entered ihram for Umrah and Hajj together; I passed by al-Udhayb by Salman ibn Rabiah and Zayd ibn Suhan, and they heard me while I was entering ihram for both together. One of them said to his companion: With both together? And the other said: Leave him, for he is more astray than his camel. He said: So I went on, with my camel upon my neck. I came to Madinah and met Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and related to him, and he said: They said nothing; you have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet."
"3452","3683 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali narrated to us, he said: Waki informed me, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, from Shaqiq, from al-Subayy ibn Mabad, he said: I entered ihram for both together; I passed by Salman ibn Rabiah and Zayd ibn Suhan, and they disapproved that of me. When I came to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, I mentioned that to him, and he said: They said nothing; you have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So his saying: You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet, after his saying: They said nothing, indicates that was from him by way of approval, not supplication. And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, what also indicates that."
"3453","3684 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while at al-Aqiq, saying: There came to me tonight one coming from my Lord, saying: Pray in this blessed valley, and say: Umrah in Hajj."
"3454","3685 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, then he mentioned with his isnad the like of it. So Umar informed in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that one coming from his Lord came to him and said to him: Say: Umrah in Hajj. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been ordered to put Umrah in Hajj, it is impossible that what he did was contrary to what he was ordered. If one says: How can this be transmitted from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, while he forbade mutah? You mentioned that from him in the hadith of Malik, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal? And he mentioned in that also what:"
"3455","3686 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Two mutahs were in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I forbid them and punish for them: the mutah of women, and the mutah of Hajj."
"3456","3687 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind informed me, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to forbid the mutah of women and the mutah of Hajj. They said: How can it be allowed that he punish anyone for a matter he knew Allah the Mighty and Majestic had ordered His Messenger with? It is said to him: This mutah in this hadith is not the mutah which the people of the view we mentioned in the preceding section preferred; rather this mutah - with us, and Allah knows best - is the ihram which the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had entered ihram with for Hajj, then circumambulated for it, and ran before Arafah, and shaved and exited ihram; that is a mutah that used to be done in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then was abrogated; and we shall mention it and what was narrated in it and in its abrogation, in another place in this book of ours, if Allah the Exalted wills. So this is the mutah which Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade and threatened with punishment one who does it. As for a mutah which Allah the Mighty and Majestic mentioned in His Book by His saying: Whoever performs tamattu with Umrah to Hajj, then whatever sacrificial animal is feasible. The verse. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions did it; so it is impossible that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade it; rather we narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he preferred it and urged it."
"3457","3688 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, he said: I heard Tawus narrating from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: They say: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade mutah. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If I perform Umrah twice in a year, then perform Hajj, I would join it with my Hajj."
"3458","3689 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said, then he mentioned the like of it. So this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, denied that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade tamattu, and mentioned from him that he preferred qiran; so that indicates that the mutah which Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, threatened with punishment one who does it, is the other mutah. If one says: It is narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he ordered ifrad of Hajj, and he mentioned in that what:"
"3459","3690 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Ala, he said: I heard Suwayd say: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Perform Hajj alone. It is said to him: That is not - with us - upon his dislike for what is besides ifrad of tamattu and qiran; rather it is for his intending another meaning besides that, which Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, clarified."
"3460","3691 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us. (H). 3692 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik informed him, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Separate between your Hajj and your Umrah, for it is more complete for the Hajj of one of you, and more complete for his Umrah, that he perform Umrah in other than the months of Hajj."
"3461","3693 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: I said to Salim: Why did Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forbid mutah, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did that, and the people did it with him? He said: Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed me, that Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The most complete Umrah is that you single it out from the months of Hajj; and Hajj is known months, so devote Hajj in them purely, and perform Umrah in other than them of the months. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, intended thereby the completeness of Umrah, for the saying of Allah the Mighty and Majestic: And complete Hajj and Umrah for Allah. That is because the Umrah in which a man performs tamattu to Hajj is not complete except that its performer sacrifice a sacrificial animal, or fast if he does not find a sacrificial animal; while Umrah in other than the months of Hajj is complete without sacrificial animal or fasting. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, intended by what he ordered of that, that: the House be visited twice every year; and he disliked that people perform tamattu with Umrah to Hajj, lest people become bound to that, and only come to the House once a year. So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in this hadith, that he only ordered singling Umrah from Hajj, lest people become bound to that, and only come to the House once a year, not because he disliked tamattu as not being of the Sunnah. As for his saying: It is more complete for the Umrah and Hajj of one of you that he separate each one from its companion: what we narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from him, indicates the opposite of that. And we narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of his opinion, opposition to that as well."
"3462","3694 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Yasar and Abu Yafur narrated to us, both heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: That I perform Umrah in the first ten of Dhu al-Hijjah is dearer to me than that I perform Umrah in the remaining ten."
"3463","3695 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Yasar narrated to us, he heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: An Umrah in the first ten of Dhu al-Hijjah is dearer to me than that I perform Umrah in the remaining ten. So I narrated it to Nafi, and he said: Yes; an Umrah in which is a sacrificial animal or fasting is dearer to him than an Umrah in which is neither sacrificial animal nor fasting."
"3464","3696 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Kathir ibn Jumhan, he said: We performed Hajj and among us was a non-Arab man, and he said labbayk for Umrah and Hajj; we faulted that of him. So we asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: A man among us said labbayk for Umrah and Hajj, what is his expiation? He said: He returned with two rewards, and you return with one reward."
"3465","3697 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, that Malik narrated to him, from Sadaqah ibn Yasar, from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: By Allah, that I perform Umrah before Hajj and sacrifice is dearer to me than that I perform Umrah after Hajj in Dhu al-Hijjah. So this Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, also preferred the Umrah that is in the months of Hajj over the Umrah that is in other than the months of Hajj. So that indicates the soundness of what Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him; because Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had he heard that from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, as in the hadith of Uqayl from al-Zuhri, would not have said contrary to that, because he had heard his father say it in the presence of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with no denier denying it to him, nor any repeller repelling it from him; and he also does not repel it from him, nor say to him: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had done this. But what is related in that from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, is Umar's desire, may Allah be pleased with him, that the House be visited, and the rest of the speech after that. So the speech of Salim, al-Zuhri mixed it with his narration, so it was not distinguished. As for his saying: Umrah in the months of Hajj is not complete except with the sacrificial animal for one who finds it, or with fasting for one who does not find it. So thereby is established the completeness of Umrah in other than the months of Hajj when that is not obligatory in it, and imposed deficiency in the Umrah that is in the months of Hajj when it is obligatory in it; and all this when Hajj follows it. The proof against one who holds that - with us, and Allah knows best - is that we saw the sacrificial animal obligatory in mutah and qiran is eaten from, by agreement of all the early ones; and we saw the sacrificial animal obligatory for deficiency in Umrah or Hajj is not eaten from, by their agreement all. Since the obligatory sacrificial animal in mutah and qiran is eaten from, it is established that it is not obligatory for deficiency in Umrah, or in the Hajj after it; because had it been for deficiency it would have been of the kinds of blood obligatory for deficiency, and would not be eaten from, as they are not eaten from; rather it is blood of bounty, and attaining good."
"3466","3698 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Waki narrated to us. (H). 3699 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Khadir ibn Muhammad al-Harrani narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus and Abu Usamah informed me, all saying: from al-Amash, from Muslim al-Batin, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, he said: We were traveling with Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man was saying labbayk for Hajj and Umrah, so Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Who is this? They said: Ali. So Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him and said: Did you not know that I forbade this? He said: Yes, but I would not leave the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for your saying."
"3467","3700 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn Ata, he said: Hurayth ibn Sulaym al-Udhri narrated to me, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said labbayk for both together, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade him; so Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Verily you have seen. So this Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, informed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contrary to the prohibition of qiran of Umrah and Hajj, and did in that contrary to what Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, ordered, and objected to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, what he ordered of that; so this from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, indicates that he had with him preference of qiran over ifrad from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); were it not that, he would not have objected to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, what he saw, nor preferred his opinion over the opinion of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, in that, since both of them only ordered with what they ordered of that from one thing, which is opinion. But his opposing Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, in that is evidence - with us - that he knew the excellence of qiran over what is besides it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, also, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qiran in the Farewell Hajj."
"3468","3701 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed four Umrahs: the Umrah of al-Juhfah, his Umrah from the following year, his Umrah from al-Jiranah, and his Umrah with his Hajj; and he performed one Hajj. If one says: How do you accept this from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, while you narrated from him in the first section that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu? It is said to him: It is possible the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram at the beginning of his affair for Umrah, and proceeded therein as mutamatti with it, then entered ihram for Hajj before his tawaf, so at the beginning of his affair he was mutamatti, and at its end qarin. So Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed in the first hadith of the tamattu of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to negate the saying of one who disliked mutah; and informed in this second hadith of his qiran upon what his affair had become after his ihram for Hajj. So thereby it is established that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been in the Farewell Hajj mutamatti after his ihram for Umrah, until he entered ihram for Hajj, so became thereby qarin."
"3469","3702 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Nufayli narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Umar was asked: How many Umrahs did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform? He said: Twice. So Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, well knows that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed three Umrahs besides his Umrah which he joined with his Hajj. If one says: How do you accept the like of this from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her? While you narrated from her at the beginning of this chapter what you narrated of the ifrad of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his tamattu upon what you mentioned? It is said to him: That is with us - and Allah knows best - upon the like of what we validated the hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, upon: so what Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, knew of the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is that he began and entered ihram for Umrah, and did not join it then with Hajj; so he proceeded in it upon performing Hajj at the time of Hajj, so was in that mutamatti with it; then he entered ihram for Hajj alone in his ihram for it, not beginning with it ihram for Umrah, so became thereby qarin joining it to his preceding Umrah. So he had been in his ihram upon different things: at its beginning mutamatti, then became entering ihram for Hajj which he singled in his ihram, so it became binding with the Umrah he had put before; so he became in the meaning of qarin and mutamatti; and she meant - meaning Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her - by her mentioning ifrad, opposition to those who hold that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for both together."
"3470","3703 - And Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar set out from Madinah to Makkah entering ihram for Umrah, fearing prevention; then he said: Their matter is but one; I call you to witness that I have added to this Umrah of mine a Hajj. Then he came and circumambulated for both one tawaf, and said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did."
"3471","3704 - And Ahmad - he is Ibn Dawud ibn Musa - narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd ibn Kasib narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, intended Hajj in the year al-Hajjaj descended upon Ibn al-Zubayr, so he entered ihram for Umrah; it was said to him: The people will have fighting among them, and we fear they will prevent you from the House. He said: There has been for you in the Messenger of Allah a good example; then I shall do as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did; I call you to witness that I have made obligatory an Umrah. Then he set out until when he was at the back of al-Bayda he said: The matter of Hajj and Umrah is but one; I call you to witness that I have made obligatory a Hajj with my Umrah. So he went on entering ihram for both together until he came to Makkah; he circumambulated the House, and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and did not add to that; he did not sacrifice, nor shave, nor exit ihram from anything forbidden to him, until the Day of Sacrifice; then he shaved and held that he had discharged the tawaf of Hajj with that first tawaf of his; then he said: Thus the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did."
"3472","3705 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from al-Layth, from Nafi, that Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, intended Hajj in the year al-Hajjaj descended upon Ibn al-Zubayr. It was said to him: The people will have fighting among them, and we fear they will prevent you from the House. He said: There has been for you in the Messenger of Allah a good example; then I shall do as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did; I call you to witness that I have made obligatory a Hajj with my Umrah. Then he set out until when he was at the back of al-Bayda he said: The matter of Hajj and Umrah is but one; I call you to witness that I have made obligatory a Hajj with my Umrah; and he drove a sacrificial animal he had bought at Qudayd. So he went on entering ihram for both together until he came to Makkah; he circumambulated the House and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and did not add to that; he did not sacrifice, nor shave, nor shorten, nor exit ihram from anything forbidden to him, until it was the Day of Sacrifice; then he sacrificed, and shaved, and held that he had discharged the tawaf of Hajj and Umrah with his first tawaf; and likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did. If one says: How do you accept the like of this from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, while you narrated from him above that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu? Our answer to him in that is like our answer to him in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"3473","3706 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from Mutarrif ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Imran ibn al-Husayn, that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying labbayk for Umrah and Hajj. If one says: You narrated from Imran also above in this chapter that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu, so how do you accept from him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qiran? Our answer to him in that is like our answer in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"3474","3707 - And Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said labbayk for Umrah and Hajj, saying: Labbayk for Umrah and Hajj. Bakr ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani mentioned to Ibn Umar the saying of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: Anas became confused; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only entered ihram for Hajj, and we entered ihram for it with him; when we came to Makkah he said: Whoever has no sacrificial animal with him, let him exit ihram. Bakr said: So I returned to Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, and informed him of the saying of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he kept mentioning that until he died."
"3475","3708 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiyah narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, he said: And Bakr ibn Abd Allah narrated to me, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it. Bakr said: So I mentioned that to Ibn Umar, and he said: Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, became confused; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only entered ihram for Hajj, and we entered ihram for it."
"3476","3709 - Husayn - he is Ibn Nasr - narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Humayd informed me, then he mentioned the like of it with his isnad, adding: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came he said: Whoever has no sacrificial animal with him, let him exit ihram; and there was a sacrificial animal with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he did not exit ihram."
"3477","3710 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Bakr, he said: I informed Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of the saying of Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, forgot. When he returned Bakr said to Anas: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, says: He forgot. He said: Do you count us except as children; rather I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Labbayk for Umrah and Hajj together. Do you not see that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, only denied Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, his saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for both together? The matter with Ibn Umar was that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for Hajj, then made it Umrah after that, and added to it Hajj, so became then qarin. As for at the beginning of his ihram, he was - with him - mufrid; then successive narrations came after that from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, of the entering of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) into both together."
"3478","3711 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Hibban narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when his mount stood straight with him upon al-Bayda, joined between them."
"3479","3712 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him. (H). 3713 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Qazaah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Labbayk for Umrah and Hajj."
"3480","3714 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3481","3715 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3482","3716 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr, he is al-Raqqi, narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilaba and Humayd ibn Hilal, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was riding behind Abu Talha, and my knee was touching the knee of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they kept crying out with both of them together, with Hajj and Umra."
"3483","3717 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, he said: I heard Anas saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Labbayk with an Umra and a Hajj together."
"3484","3718 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Asim al-Kilabi narrated to us. (H). 3719 - And Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, they both said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed an Umra from al-Juhfa, and an Umra in the following year, and an Umra from al-Jirana, and an Umra where he distributed the spoils of Hunayn, and an Umra along with his Hajj, and he performed one Hajj."
"3485","3720 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Musa and Ibn Nufayl narrated to us, they said: Abu Khaythama narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Asma, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We went out crying out for Hajj, and when we arrived at Mecca the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to make it an Umra, and he said: If I had known beforehand what I have come to know afterwards, I would have made it an Umra, but I have driven the sacrificial animal, and I have combined Hajj and Umra. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith, from the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he combined Hajj and Umra, there is proof of the correctness of the statement of those who reported from his deed what agrees with that."
"3486","3721 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us. (H). 3722 - And Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb narrated to us, they both said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Aslam Abu Imran, that he said: I performed Hajj with my masters, and I entered upon Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with her, and I heard her saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Enter ihram, O family of Muhammad, with an Umra within a Hajj. And this is also like that."
"3487","3723 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid and Abu Muawiya narrated to us. (H). 3724 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, all of them said: from al-Hajjaj, from al-Hasan ibn Sad, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Abu Talha, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined Hajj and Umra."
"3488","3725 - Abu Bakra and Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, they said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Yazid al-Awdi narrated to us, he said: I heard Abdulmalik ibn Maysara al-Zarrad, he said: I heard al-Nazzal ibn Sabra saying: I heard Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Jushum saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Umra has entered into Hajj until the Day of Judgment. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) combined in the Farewell Hajj. They differed from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his ihram in the Farewell Hajj, what it was, and they said what we have narrated, and they disputed in that as we have mentioned. And our knowledge has encompassed that it could only have been upon one of those three states: either mutamatti, or mufrid, or qarin. So it is most proper for us to look at the meanings of these reports and uncover them, to know whence their disagreement came, and to establish thereby what his ihram (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was. So we considered that, and we found those who say he performed ifrad saying: his ihram was for Hajj alone, with no ihram before that for anything else. And others said: rather, before his ihram for that Hajj he had entered ihram for Umra, then added this Hajj to it; thus say those who said he performed qiran. And Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, informed in his hadith, and he is one of those who said the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ifrad, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered ihram for Hajj when his she-camel rose with him upon al-Bayda. And Ibn Umar said: from the mosque, and he is also among those who said the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ifrad of Hajj at the beginning of his ihram. So the start of his ihram, upon him be peace, according to Ibn Umar and Jabir, may Allah be pleased with them, was after his leaving the mosque. And we have already explained from him in what preceded in this book of ours that he had entered ihram at the end of the prayer in the mosque. So it is possible that those who said he performed qiran heard his talbiya in the mosque for Umra, then heard after that his other talbiya, outside the mosque, for Hajj alone, so they knew that he performed qiran; and those who said he performed ifrad heard him when he called out the talbiya for Hajj alone, and had not heard his talbiya before that for Umra, so they said: he performed ifrad; and some people also heard him when he called out the talbiya in the mosque for Umra, and did not hear his talbiya after leaving it for Hajj, then saw him after that doing what the pilgrim does of standing at Arafa and the like, and that was, in their view, after his exit from Umra, so they said: he performed tamattu; so each group narrated what it knew. And all that those who said ifrad knew, and what those who said he performed tamattu knew, is included in what those who said he performed qiran knew, for they reported of his talbiya for Umra, then of his talbiya for Hajj immediately after that. So what they held of that and what they narrated is more worthy than what those who opposed them held and narrated. Then we found after that the deeds of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proving that he was a qarin, and that is that there is no disagreement from him that when he arrived at Mecca he commanded his Companions to exit ihram except those among them who had driven a sacrificial animal, while he remained upon his ihram, and did not exit from it except at the time when the pilgrim exits from his Hajj. And he said: Had I known beforehand what I have come to know afterwards, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal and I would have made it an Umra; so whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him, let him exit ihram and make it an Umra. Thus Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated from him, and he is among those who say he performed ifrad, and we shall mention that and what was narrated therein in the chapter of faskh al-Hajj, Allah willing. So had that ihram of his been for Hajj, his sacrificial animal which he drove would have been voluntary, and voluntary sacrificial animals do not prevent the exiting of ihram which a man performs when he has no sacrificial animal with him, and his ruling (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), though he had driven a sacrificial animal, would have been like the ruling of one who had not driven a sacrificial animal, for he had not set out to perform tamattu, so that that sacrificial animal would be for tamattu and would prevent him from the exiting of ihram which he would have performed had he not driven a sacrificial animal. Do you not see that had a man set out intending tamattu and entered ihram for Umra, then when he circumambulates for it, runs, and shaves, he exits from it, and had he driven a sacrificial animal for his tamattu he would not exit until the Day of Sacrifice, while had he driven a voluntary sacrificial animal he would exit before the Day of Sacrifice after finishing Umra. So it is established thereby that the sacrificial animal of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), since it prevented him from exiting ihram and necessitated his remaining upon ihram until the Day of Sacrifice, its ruling is other than the ruling of voluntary sacrificial animals, and so the statement of one who said he was a mufrid is negated. And we have mentioned in what preceded in this chapter from Hafsa that she said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What is the matter with the people, they have exited ihram, and you have not exited from your Umra? So he said: I have garlanded my sacrificial animal and matted my head, so I shall not exit until I sacrifice. So that proves what we mentioned, and that that sacrificial animal was a sacrificial animal by reason of an Umra intended for qiran or tamattu. So we considered that, and the hadith of Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, proves that that statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in Mecca, for it was from him after the people had exited ihram. And it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had performed tawaf before that or had not performed tawaf. If he had performed tawaf before that, then entered ihram for Hajj afterwards, then he was a mutamatti and was not a qarin, for he only entered ihram for Hajj after finishing the tawaf of Umra. And if he had not performed tawaf before that until he entered ihram for Hajj, then he was a qarin, for Hajj had become binding upon him before his tawaf for Umra. Since that admits what we mentioned, the most proper thing for us is to carry these reports upon that in which they agree, not upon that in which they contradict. And Ali ibn Abi Talib, Ibn Abbas, Imran ibn Husayn, and Aisha, may Allah be pleased with them, we have narrated from them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed tamattu, and we have narrated from them that he performed qiran, and it is established from his statement what proves that he arrived at Mecca and had not entered ihram for Hajj before that; so if we make his ihram for Hajj to have been before the tawaf for Umra, both hadiths are established, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been a mutamatti until he entered ihram for Hajj, then became a qarin. And if we make his ihram for Hajj to have been after his tawaf for Umra, we make him a mutamatti and negate that he was a qarin. So it is established thereby that his tawaf for Umra was after his ihram for Hajj, and it is established thereby that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the Farewell Hajj a qarin. Then one who dislikes qiran and tamattu said to those who approve them: you have argued against us by the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: (Whoever performs tamattu with Umra to Hajj, then whatever sacrificial animal is feasible), for the permissibility of tamattu, but that is not so; rather the interpretation of this verse is what was narrated from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr. Then he mentioned what."
"3489","3726 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj and Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr while he was preaching, saying: O people, by Allah, tamattu with Umra to Hajj is not as you do it; rather tamattu with Umra to Hajj is that a man goes out intending Hajj, then an enemy detains him, or an illness, or a matter for which he is excused, until the days of Hajj pass, then he comes to the House and circumambulates it seven times, and runs between al-Safa and al-Marwa, and enjoys his state of freedom until the next year, then he performs Hajj and sacrifices."
"3490","3727 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Suwayd informed me, and he mentioned the like of it. He said: So this is the interpretation of this verse. It was said to them: If it must be that its interpretation is so because of the statement of Ibn al-Zubayr, then its interpretation is more likely not to be so, because of what we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from his Companions after him, such as Umar and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, and those we mentioned along with them in what preceded in this chapter."
"3491","3728 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, or Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu Nasr, he said: I entered ihram for Hajj, then I met Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and I said: I have entered ihram for Hajj, can I add to it? He said: No; had you entered ihram for Umra, then wanted to add Hajj to it, you could have done so."
"3492","3729 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, he said: We were with Uthman ibn Affan, and we heard a man crying out for Hajj and Umra, so Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Who is this? They said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, so he fell silent."
"3493","3730 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Jurayy ibn Kulayb and Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, preached and forbade tamattu, so Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and called out the talbiya for both of them, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, disapproved of that, so Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: The best of us in this matter is the most adherent of us to him."
"3494","3731 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Bishr narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Yashkuri, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Had I entered ihram for Hajj and Umra, I would have performed one tawaf for both of them, and I would have brought a sacrificial animal. Abu Jafar said: So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whom we have mentioned have directed the interpretation of the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: (Whoever performs tamattu with Umra to Hajj, then whatever sacrificial animal is feasible), to other than what Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr directed it to, and it is the more correct of the two interpretations in our view, and Allah knows best; for in the verse there is what proves the invalidity of the interpretation of Ibn al-Zubayr, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: (Whoever performs tamattu with Umra to Hajj, then whatever sacrificial animal is feasible, and whoever does not find, then fasting three days during Hajj). And fasting during Hajj cannot be after the passing of Hajj, but it is before its passing. Then He said: (And seven when you return, that is ten complete; that is for one whose family is not present at the Sacred Mosque). So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, only made tamattu, and obligated therein what He obligated upon one who does it, when his family is not present at the Sacred Mosque. And the umma has unanimously agreed that one whose family is present at the Sacred Mosque, or not present at the Sacred Mosque, and Hajj passes him by, his ruling in that and the ruling of another are the same, and that his state with his family being present at the Sacred Mosque does not differ from his state with their being distant from the Sacred Mosque. So it is established thereby that the tamattu which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, mentioned in this verse is that in which one whose family is in the vicinity of the Sacred Mosque differs from one whose family is not in the vicinity of the Sacred Mosque, and that is tamattu with Umra to Hajj which our opponent disliked."
"3495","3732 - And Abdullah ibn Abbas narrated in that, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, he said: They used to consider Umra in the months of Hajj among the most wicked of wicked deeds. He said: And they used to call Muharram Safar, and they used to say: When the back of the camel heals, and the trace is effaced, and Safar has passed, Umra becomes lawful for one who performs Umra. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived with his Companions on the morning of the fourth, while they were calling out the talbiya for Hajj, so he commanded them to make it an Umra. They said: O Messenger of Allah, with what release do we become free? He said: The complete release. So this Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, has informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only converted Hajj into Umra in order to teach the people the opposite of what they used to dislike in the Jahiliyya, and so that they would know that Umra in the months of Hajj is permissible, as it is in other than the months of Hajj. If one who says says: It is established by this from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the ihram of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only with a single Hajj, so this has contradicted what you have narrated from him of the tamattu and qiran of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). It is said to him: There is no contradiction in this to that; for it is possible that his ihram at first was with a Hajj until he arrived at Mecca, then he converted that into an Umra, then he remained upon it as an Umra, having resolved to enter ihram after it with a Hajj, so in that he was a mutamatti, then he did not perform tawaf for Umra until he entered ihram for Hajj, so by that he became a qarin. So these aspects of the hadiths of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, are sound and coherent, upon that qiran was preceded by tamattu and ifrad, so they do not contradict, except that in his statement: Had I not driven the sacrificial animal I would have exited ihram as my Companions exited, there is proof that his driving of the sacrificial animal was at a time when he had entered ihram with an Umra, intending thereby tamattu to Hajj; for had he not done that, his sacrificial animal would have been voluntary, and voluntary sacrificial animals do not prevent the exiting of ihram which would occur had there been no sacrificial animal. So that proves that the ihram of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was at first with an Umra, then he followed it with a Hajj in the manner we mentioned in what preceded in this chapter. And when the permissibility of Umra in the months of Hajj was established by what we described, we wanted to consider whether the sacrificial animal obligatory in qiran was for a deficiency that entered Umra or Hajj when they were combined or not. And we saw that sacrificial animal is eaten from, and likewise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did."
"3496","3733 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, in the long hadith, he said: And Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, had arrived from Yemen with sacrificial animals for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the total of the sacrificial animals which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Ali brought from Yemen was one hundred camels; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughtered sixty-three of them with his own hand, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, slaughtered thirty-seven, and he made Ali a partner in his sacrificial animal. Then he took from every camel a piece, and it was placed in a pot and cooked, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, ate of its meat and drank of its broth. Since it is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by what we mentioned before this section that he performed qiran, and that a sacrificial animal was due upon him for that, then he sacrificed these camels which we mentioned, and ate from every camel what we described, it is established thereby that eating from the sacrificial animal of tamattu and qiran is permissible. Since that sacrificial animal is of what is eaten from, we considered the ruling of the bloods obligatory for deficiency, whether they are so or not. And we saw the blood obligatory for clipping nails, shaving hair, intercourse, and every blood obligatory for abandoning something of Hajj, none of that is eaten from; so every blood obligatory for an offense or deficiency is not eaten from, while the blood of tamattu and qiran is eaten from. So it is established thereby that they are obligatory for a reason other than offense and deficiency, and this is a decisive proof against one who dislikes qiran and tamattu with Umra to Hajj. Then the discussion after that between those who permit tamattu and qiran is about the preference of some of them of qiran over tamattu, and the preference of others of tamattu over qiran. So we considered that, and in qiran there is hastening the ihram for Hajj, while in tamattu there is delaying it; so what is hastened of the ihram for Hajj is better and more complete for that ihram."
"3497","3734 - And it has been narrated from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: (And complete Hajj and Umra for Allah). He said: Completing them is to enter ihram for them from the vicinity of your family. Ibn Marzuq narrated to us of that, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shuba, from Amr ibn Murra, from Abdullah ibn Salima, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Since in qiran there is advancing the ihram for Hajj before the time in which one enters ihram in tamattu, qiran is better than tamattu. And all that we have established and authenticated in this chapter is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them."
"3498","Chapter: The sacrificial animal driven for tamattu or qiran, may it be ridden or not? 3735 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man driving a camel, and he said: Ride it. He said: O Messenger of Allah, it is a sacrificial camel. He said: Ride it, woe to you."
"3499","3736 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Ajlan, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3500","3737 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from his uncle Musa ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Except that he said to him on the third or the fourth time: Ride it, woe to you."
"3501","3738 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad, he is Ibn Salama, narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man driving a sacrificial camel, and he said: Ride it. He said: It is a sacrificial camel. He said: Ride it."
"3502","3739 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Uthman, from his father, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3503","3740 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Mutamir narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ikrima, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he saw a man driving a sacrificial camel. He said: Ride it. He said: It is a sacrificial camel. He said: Ride it with its rope which is on its neck. He said: I saw him walking alongside the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a sandal on its neck."
"3504","3741 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Hajjaj ibn Artat, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a man driving a sacrificial camel, and he said: Ride it; and you are not following a Sunnah better guided than the Sunnah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3505","3742 - Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil informed me, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man while he was driving a sacrificial camel, and he said: Ride it. He said: It is a sacrificial camel. He said: Ride it."
"3506","3743 - Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham and Shuba narrated to us, they said: Qatada narrated to us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So some people held that when a man drives a camel for tamattu or qiran, he may ride it, and they argued for that with these reports. Others opposed them in that and said: This was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only because of a hardship he saw in the man, so he commanded him with what he commanded him for that. And thus we say: there is no harm in riding it in case of necessity, and it is not permitted in case of sufficiency. It is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded that because of necessity as they said, and it is possible that that was not for necessity, but because the ruling of all sacrificial camels is so: they are ridden in case of necessity and in case of sufficiency. So we examined that."
"3507","3744 - So Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Humayd, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man driving a sacrificial camel while he was exhausted, and he said: Ride it. He said: O Messenger of Allah, it is a sacrificial camel. He said: Ride it."
"3508","3745 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan and al-Nufayli narrated to us, they said: Zuhayr ibn Muawiya narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man driving a sacrificial camel, and it was as if he saw exhaustion in him, so he said: Ride it. He said: It is a sacrificial camel. He said: Ride it, even if it is a sacrificial camel. And there is narrated in the hadith of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, a wording also proving this meaning."
"3509","3746 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say concerning a man when he drives a sacrificial camel and becomes exhausted: Ride it; and you are not following a Sunnah better guided than the Sunnah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So that also proves that what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded, and what he reported to be the Sunnah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is the riding of the sacrificial camel in case of necessity. Then we sought the ruling of riding sacrificial animals in other than case of necessity, whether we find mention of it in other than these reports."
"3510","3747 - So Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ride sacrificial animals in a fitting manner, until you find a back to ride."
"3511","3748 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us. (H). 3749 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, they said: Ibn Lahia narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning riding sacrificial animals: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Ride it in a fitting manner when you are compelled to it, until you find a back to ride. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith permitted riding it in case of necessity, and forbade that when necessity is lifted and another mount is found. So it is established thereby that this is the ruling of sacrificial animals by way of reports: they are ridden for necessities and left when necessities are lifted. Then we considered its ruling by way of reasoning, how it is. And we saw things are of two kinds: among them what ownership in it is complete, with nothing entering it to remove anything of the rulings of ownership, like the slave whom his master has not made mudabbar, and like the bondwoman who has not borne a child to her master, and like the sacrificial camel which its owner has not made obligatory; all that may be sold, benefited from, and its benefits may be transferred with compensation or without compensation. And among them what something has entered that prevents its sale, but the ruling of benefiting from it has not ceased, such as the mother of the child whom her master may not sell, and the mudabbar according to one who does not hold its sale permissible; there is no harm in benefiting from it and in transferring its benefits to one who wishes to benefit by them, with compensation or without compensation. Then we saw that when its owner makes the sacrificial camel obligatory, all agree that he may not hire it out nor take compensation for its benefits. Since he may not transfer its benefits with compensation, likewise he may not benefit from it himself. And this is also the reasoning, and it is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy upon them. And that has been narrated from a group of the predecessors."
"3512","3750 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, I think from Mughira, from Ibrahim, he said: One does not drink the milk of the sacrificial camel, nor ride it, except when compelled to that."
"3513","3751 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Urwa narrated to us, from his father, he said: When its driver needs the sacrificial camel, he rides it a riding that is not exhausting."
"3514","3752 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qays, from Ata, the like of it."
"3515","It has been narrated from the predecessors concerning the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: (For you therein are benefits for an appointed term), what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Shuba, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid. H."
"3516","3753 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, from Sufyan and Hibban, from Hammad, both of them from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: (For you therein are benefits for an appointed term). He said: In their backs, their milk, their wool, and their fur, until they become sacrificial camels."
"3517","3754 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Najih informed me, from Mujahid: (For you therein are benefits for an appointed term). He said: They are camels benefited from until they are garlanded."
"3518","3755 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Warqa narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim: (For you therein are benefits for an appointed term). He said: If he needs its back he rides, and if he needs its milk he drinks, meaning sacrificial camels."
"3519","Chapter: What the muhrim may kill of animals. 3756 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, from Muhammad ibn al-Ajlan, from al-Qaqa ibn Hakim, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of the hadith of Malik and al-Layth, meaning that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five animals are killed in the Sanctuary: the scorpion, the kite, the crow, the mouse, and the vicious dog. Except that he said in his hadith: and the snake, the wolf, and the vicious dog."
"3520","3757 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfa narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The vicious dog is the lion."
"3521","3758 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Maysara narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from Ibn Silan, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So some people held to this and said: The vicious dog which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted killing is the lion, and every vicious beast of prey is included in that. Others opposed them in that and said: The vicious dog is the well-known dog, and the lion is not of it in anything. And they said: There is not in the hadith of Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the vicious dog is the lion; that is only from the statement of Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him. And we have found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also what repels that, and it is what."
"3522","3759 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bakr al-Bursani narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr informed me that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar informed him, he said: I asked Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, about the hyena, and I said: May I eat it? He said: Yes. I said: Is it game? He said: Yes. I said: And did you hear that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes."
"3523","3760 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hibban, Shayban, and Hudba narrated to us, they said: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us. (H). 3761 - And Ali ibn Shayba narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us. (H). 3762 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Ammar narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the hyena, and he said: It is of game, and he assigned for it, when the muhrim hits it, a ram."
"3524","3763 - Harun ibn Kamil narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Ayyub, he said: Ismail ibn Umayya, Ibn Jurayj, and Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to me that Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah ibn Umayr narrated to them, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ammar narrated to me that he asked Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, about the hyena, and he said: May I eat it? He said: Yes. I said: Is it game? He said: Yes. I said: Did you hear that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes."
"3525","3764 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Hibban narrated to us. (H). 3765 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, they said: Hassan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Saigh, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. And he added: And he assigned for it, when the muhrim hits it, an aged ram, and it is eaten."
"3526","3766 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Zadham, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: It was judged for the hyena, when the muhrim kills it, with a ram. Since the hyena is a beast of prey, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not permit its killing, and made it game, and placed compensation upon its killer, that proves to us that the vicious dog is not the beast of prey, and what Abu Hurayra held is thereby nullified; and the vicious dog is the dog which the common people know. If one who says says: Then why do you not permit killing the wolf? It is said to him: Because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five animals are killed in the lawful land and the Sanctuary. Then he mentioned what the five are. So the mention of five proves that other than the five has a ruling other than theirs, otherwise there would be no meaning in his mentioning five. So those who permitted killing the wolf permitted killing all beasts of prey, and those who forbade killing the wolf prohibited killing the rest of the beasts of prey, other than the vicious dog specifically. And it is established that the hyena is excluded from killing, though it is not a vicious dog, and it is established that the vicious dog is the dog which the common people know. As for what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what is killed in ihram and the Sanctuary, it is what."
"3527","3767 - Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi and Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, he said: Hafsa said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five animals the muhrim kills: the crow, the kite, the mouse, the scorpion, and the vicious dog."
"3528","3768 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zura narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, that Abdullah ibn Umar said: Hafsa, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3529","3769 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Jubayr narrated to us, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man asked Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, about what the muhrim kills. So he said: One of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me that he used to command, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3530","3770 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: What does the muhrim kill? Then he mentioned the like of it."
"3531","3771 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us. (H). 3772 - And Yazid narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3532","3773 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3533","3774 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3534","3775 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Malik informed me, from Nafi and Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3535","3776 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3536","3777 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Qanabi narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3537","3778 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, Shuba said: I said: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes, and it is transmitted the like of it."
"3538","3779 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Said ibn al-Musayyab, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3539","3780 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Except that he said: The spotted crow."
"3540","3781 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwa, from his father, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Five harmful creatures are killed in the lawful land and the Sanctuary: the vicious dog, the mouse, the kite, the crow, and the scorpion."
"3541","3782 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ibn Abi Num, from Abu Said al-Khudri, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The muhrim kills the snake, the scorpion, and the mischievous mouse. Yazid said: And he counted other than this, but I did not memorize. He said: I said: And why was the mouse called mischievous? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) awoke one night, and a mouse had taken a wick to burn the house upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So he rose to it and killed it, and he permitted its killing for every muhrim or non-muhrim. So this is what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the muhrim to kill in his ihram, and permitted the non-muhrim to kill in the Sanctuary, and he counted that as five. So that negates that the ruling of the likes of any of that is like the ruling of these five, except what is agreed upon of that which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant. If one who says says: We have seen the snake permitted to be killed in all that, and likewise all vermin, yet the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned of that the scorpion specifically, and you have made all vermin like it, so what do you deny that beasts of prey are likewise, so that what was mentioned of permitting killing of them is permission of the like for killing all of them? It is said to him: We have shown you from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) an explicit text concerning the hyena, and it is of beasts of prey, that it is not included in what he permitted killing of the five. So it is established thereby that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not intend the killing of the rest of the beasts of prey by his permitting the killing of the vicious dog, but he intended thereby a specific one of the beasts of prey. Then we have seen him also permit along with that the killing of the crow and the kite, and they are of taloned birds, and they have unanimously agreed that he did not intend thereby every taloned bird; for they have unanimously agreed that the eagle, the falcon, and the hawk are taloned, and that they are not killed in the Sanctuary as the crow and the kite are killed. Rather the permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for killing the crow and the kite is upon them specifically, not upon other than them of every taloned bird. And they have unanimously agreed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted killing the scorpion in ihram and the Sanctuary. And they have unanimously agreed that all vermin are like it, and that the intent of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by permitting the killing of the scorpion is permitting the killing of all vermin. So the fanged of beasts of prey resembles the taloned of birds more than it resembles vermin, along with what has clarified that and strengthened it which Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of the hyena. If one who says says: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made the ruling of the hyena as you mentioned only because it is eaten; as for what is not eaten of beasts of prey, it is like the dog. It is said to him: You have erred in analogy; for we have seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permit the killing of the crow, the kite, and the mouse, and the eating of the meat of these is permissible in your view, yet the permissibility of eating them did not necessitate the prohibition of killing them. So likewise the hyena: the permissibility of eating it did not necessitate the prohibition of killing it; rather what prevented its killing is that it is game, though it is a beast of prey. So all beasts of prey are likewise, except the dog which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled out with what he singled it out. If one who says says: Then how can the rest of the beasts of prey be so, while they are not eaten? It is said to him: There may be of game what is not eaten, and it is permissible for a man to hunt it to feed his dogs, when it is in the lawful land while he is non-muhrim. And there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning killing the snake also in the Sanctuary what."
"3542","3783 - Abu Umayya narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hafs narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us to kill the snake while we were at Mina. So that proves that the rest of the vermin may be killed in ihram and the Sanctuary. And all that we have authenticated in this chapter is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them, except the wolf, for they made it in that like the dog."
"3543","Chapter: Game slaughtered by a non-muhrim in the lawful land, may the muhrim eat of it or not? 3784 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 3785 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, they said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Ishaq ibn Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, from his father, that Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, stayed at Qudayd, and partridges were brought to him in bowls, raised by their legs, so he sent to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and he came while colocynth leaves were falling from his hands. So Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, refrained, and the people refrained. Then Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Who here is of Ashja? Do you know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought by a Bedouin eggs and tamir, meaning wild donkeys, and he said: Feed them to your family, for we are in ihram? They said: Yes. Abu Jafar said: So some people held to this hadith and said: It is not lawful for the muhrim to eat the meat of game which a non-muhrim has slaughtered, for the game itself is unlawful to him, so its meat is also unlawful to him. And they argued in that also with what."
"3544","3786 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Imran narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought the meat of game while he was in ihram, and he did not eat it."
"3545","3787 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Qays ibn Muslim al-Jadali, from al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given dried meat of a gazelle while he was in ihram, and he returned it. Yunus said: I heard all of it from Sufyan, except his statement washika, for I did not understand that from him, and one of our companions narrated it to me from him. And there is no mention in this hadith of the cause of his returning the game meat, what it was. So it is possible that that was because of ihram, and possible that it was for other than that, so there is no proof in this hadith for anyone. And there has been narrated from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, of her own view concerning game which a non-muhrim hunts and slaughters, that there is no harm in the muhrim eating it."
"3546","3788 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, he said: A shaykh like the best of shaykhs, called Ubaydullah ibn Imran al-Qurayi, narrated to me, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Shammas saying: I came to Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, and I asked her about game meat which a non-muhrim hunts then gives to the muhrim. So she said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed about it; among them were those who forbade it, and among them were those who permitted it, and I see no harm in any of it."
"3547","3789 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Imran ibn Ubaydullah, or Ubaydullah ibn Imran, a man of Banu Tamim, from Abdullah ibn Shammas, from Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it. So this Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, the returning by the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of game meat to the non-muhrim was not in her view upon what had proved to her its prohibition for the muhrim. And they argued in that also with what."
"3548","3790 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said to Zayd ibn Arqam: You narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given a limb of game while he was in ihram, and he did not accept it."
"3549","3791 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Hasan ibn Muslim, from Tawus, he said: When Zayd ibn Arqam arrived, Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, came to him and said: A man gave to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) game meat, and he returned it, and said: I am in ihram."
"3550","3792 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Qays, from Ata, that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said to Zayd ibn Arqam: Do you know that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was given a limb of game while he was in ihram, and he did not accept it? He said: Yes. So this is also like the hadith of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in it is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only returned that limb to the one who gave it to him because he was in ihram. And they argued in that also with what."
"3551","3793 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Sab ibn Jaththama, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me while I was at al-Abwa and Waddan, and I gave him the meat of a wild donkey, and he returned it to me; then when he saw dislike in my face, he said: It is not a rejection of you, but we are in ihram."
"3552","3794 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Rashid, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So it was said to them: This is a confused hadith; some people narrated it as we mentioned, and others narrated it and said: He only gave him a wild donkey."
"3553","3795 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that al-Sab ibn Jaththama gave to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey, then he mentioned the like of his hadith from Sufyan."
"3554","3796 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib informed me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3555","3797 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shuayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from his father, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So in these hadiths is that the gift which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to al-Sab because he was in ihram was a wild donkey. If that is so, then no one disagrees about its prohibition for the muhrim. Except that Said ibn Jubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated this hadith from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and added therein a wording over what Ubaydullah narrated, making clear by that wording that the donkey was slaughtered."
"3556","3798 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Hudhayl, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that al-Sab ibn Jaththama gave to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey, and he returned it, and it was slaughtered."
"3557","3799 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that al-Sab ibn Jaththama gave to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a wild donkey dripping blood, and he returned it to him, and said: I am in ihram. So in this hadith is that that was slaughtered, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned it because he was in ihram. And there has also been narrated from Said ibn Jubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that it was the rump of a wild donkey or a thigh of a donkey."
"3558","3800 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir and Wahb narrated to me, from Shuba, from al-Hakam, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that al-Sab ibn Jaththama gave to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the rump of a wild donkey, while he was at Qudayd, dripping blood, and he returned it."
"3559","3801 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: I heard Mansur from al-Hakam ibn Utayba, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Except that he said: A leg of a donkey."
"3560","3802 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from al-Hakam and Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that al-Sab ibn Jaththama gave to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of them said: the rump of a donkey, and the other said: the thigh of a wild donkey, dripping blood, and he returned it. So these reports narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the hadith of al-Sab from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about his returning the gift to him agree that it was concerning the meat of game not alive; so that is proof for one who dislikes for the muhrim the eating of game meat, and that the one who hunted and slaughtered it was a non-muhrim. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the opposite of that."
"3561","3803 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Abd al-Rahman and Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Salim informed me, from Amr, freedman of al-Muttalib, from al-Muttalib ibn Abdullah ibn Hantab, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Game meat is lawful for you while you are in ihram, so long as you do not hunt it yourselves, or it is not hunted for you."
"3562","3803 (M) - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from a man of the Ansar, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"3563","3804 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Suwayd informed me, he said: Amr ibn Abi Amr narrated to me, from al-Muttalib, from Abu Musa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. So some people held to this and said: Every game hunted for the sake of a muhrim, even if the hunter is a non-muhrim, is unlawful for that muhrim, as is unlawful to him what he himself hunts. Others opposed them in that and said: Every game which a non-muhrim hunts, its meat is lawful for every muhrim and non-muhrim. And among the proof for them in the hadith of al-Muttalib which we mentioned is that the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): or it is hunted for you, may mean: or it is hunted for you by your command; if that is so, then they also say so: every game which a non-muhrim hunts for a muhrim by his command is unlawful for that muhrim. And there have been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hadiths coming with successive transmission concerning the permissibility of game meat which a non-muhrim has hunted for the muhrim, when he did not hunt it by his command nor with his assistance to him upon it."
"3564","3805 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir informed me, from Muadh ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Taymi, from his father Abd al-Rahman ibn Uthman, he said: We were with Talha ibn Ubaydullah while we were in ihram, and a bird was given as a gift to him while Talha was sleeping; so some of us ate and some of us refrained. Then when Talha awoke and it was placed before him, he ate it among those who ate it, and said: I ate it along with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3565","3806 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Isa ibn Talha, from Umayr ibn Salama, from a man of Bahz, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by al-Rawha, and there was a hamstrung wild donkey with an arrow in it, dead. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave it until its owner comes. Then the Bahzi came and said: O Messenger of Allah, it is my shot, so eat it. So he commanded Abu Bakr to divide it among the traveling companies while they were in ihram. Then he traveled until when he was at al-Uthaya, there was a gazelle sheltering in the shade in a hollow of a mountain, with an arrow in it, alive. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: Stand here, let no one see it until the companies pass."
"3566","3807 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim informed me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3567","3808 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Nafi ibn Yazid informed me, from Ibn al-Had, that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to him, from Isa ibn Talha, from Umayr ibn Salama al-Damri, he said: While we were traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in part of the environs of al-Rawha while he was in ihram, there was a hamstrung donkey. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave it, for its owner is about to come to it. Then a man of Bahz came, he is the one who hamstrung the donkey, and said: O Messenger of Allah, take this donkey for yourselves. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he divided it among the people. Then he mentioned the like of what is in the hadith of Yazid from Yazid ibn Harun."
"3568","3809 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So in the hadith of Talha and Umayr ibn Salama, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), is that he permitted muhrims to eat game meat which a non-muhrim undertook hunting. That contradicts the hadith of Ali, Zayd ibn Arqam, and al-Sab ibn Jaththama, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Except that these two hadiths of Talha and Umayr ibn Salama contain no proof about the ruling of game when the non-muhrim intends the muhrim by it."
"3569","3810 - So we considered that, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn al-Walid al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Ala narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Iyad ibn Abdullah, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Qatada al-Ansari for charity, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions went out while they were in ihram until they stopped at Usfan, and there was a wild donkey. He said: And Abu Qatada came while he was non-muhrim, so they lowered their heads lest they fix their eyes so that he notices; then he saw it, so he mounted his horse and took the spear, and it fell from him, so he said: Hand it to me. They said: We shall not assist you against it with anything. So he charged upon it and hamstrung it, and they began roasting of it, then they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is among us. He said: And he had gone ahead of them, so they caught up with him and asked him, and he saw no harm in that."
"3570","3811 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah informed me, he said: Amr ibn Yahya informed me, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abu Qatada, that he was upon a horse while he was non-muhrim, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions were in ihram; then he saw a wild donkey, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade them to assist him. So he charged upon it and struck down a she-donkey, and they ate of it."
"3571","3812 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab informed me, from Abdullah ibn Abi Qatada, from his father, that he was among a people in ihram while he was not in ihram, and they were traveling; then he saw a wild donkey, so he mounted his horse and struck it down. Then they came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about that, and he said: Did you point, or hunt, or kill? They said: No. He said: Then eat."
"3572","3813 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Nadr, from Nafi, freedman of Abu Qatada, from Abu Qatada ibn Ribi, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until when he was on part of the road to Mecca he stayed behind with companions of his in ihram while he was not in ihram; then he saw a wild donkey, so he mounted his horse, then asked his companions to hand him his whip, and they refused; then he asked them for his spear, and they refused; so he took it, then charged upon the donkey and killed it. Then some of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate of it and some refused. Then when they met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they asked him about that, and he said: It is only food which Allah has fed you."
"3573","3814 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, he informed him, from Abu Qatada, the like of it. And he added: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is there anything of its meat with you? So we know that Abu Qatada did not hunt it at the time he hunted it intending it for himself specially, but he intended it to be for him and for his companions who were with him. Yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted that to him and to them, and did not forbid it to them because of his intending it to be for them along with him. And in the hadith of Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked them and said: Did you point, or hunt, or kill? They said: No. He said: Then eat. So that proves that it is only unlawful to them when they do something of this, and it is not unlawful to them by other than that. And in that is proof that the meaning of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Amr, freedman of al-Muttalib: or it is hunted for you, is upon what is hunted for them by their command. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of the reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, also held this view."
"3574","3815 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Harun ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Salama narrated to us, from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man of the people of Syria asked him for a fatwa about game meat while he was in ihram, and he commanded him to eat it. He said: Then I met Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and I informed him of the man's question. He said: With what did you rule for him? I said: With eating it. He said: By Him in whose hand is my soul, had you ruled for him with other than that, I would have struck you with the stick; you are only forbidden to hunt it."
"3575","3816 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, that he heard Said ibn al-Musayyab narrating from Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of it. Except that he said: I would have done to you, threatening him."
"3576","3817 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, that he heard Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, narrating from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of it."
"3577","3818 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, would not have punished a man of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for his fatwa in this, contrary to what he holds, while what he has in that of what contradicts what he ruled is opinion. But that, in our view, and Allah knows best, is because he had taken knowledge of that from other than the direction of opinion."
"3578","3819 - Abu Bakra narrated to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that Kab asked Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about game which a non-muhrim slaughters and a muhrim eats, and Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Had you left it, I would have seen you understanding nothing. And the opponents of this view have argued in that with what."
"3579","3820 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from his father, he said: We were with Uthman and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, until when we were at such and such place, food was brought near to them. He said: I saw a bowl as if I am looking at the shanks of young partridges. Then when Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saw that, he stood, and people stood with him. He said: It was said: By Allah, we did not point, nor command, nor hunt. So it was said to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: This one and those with him stood only out of dislike for your food. So he called him and said: What did you dislike of this? So Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: (Game of the sea and its food are made lawful to you, as enjoyment for you and for travelers, and game of the land is made unlawful to you so long as you are in ihram). Then he departed. He said: So Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, held that game and its meat are unlawful for the muhrim. It was said to them: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, Talha ibn Ubaydullah, Aisha, may Allah be pleased with her, and Abu Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, opposed him in that, and the narrations from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have come successively with what agrees with what they held. And the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: (And game of the land is made unlawful to you so long as you are in ihram), may mean what is unlawful to them of it is that they hunt it. Do you not see the statement of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: (O you who believe, do not kill game while you are in ihram, and whoever of you kills it deliberately, then the recompense is the like of what he killed of livestock). So Allah the Exalted forbade them in this verse from killing game, and obligated upon them recompense for their killing it. So what we mentioned proves that what is unlawful for muhrims of game is killing it. And we have seen reasoning also proving this, and that is that they have unanimously agreed that game is made unlawful for the muhrim by ihram, and the Sanctuary makes it unlawful for the non-muhrim. And one who hunts game in the lawful land and slaughters it in the lawful land, then brings it into the Sanctuary, there is no harm in his eating it in the Sanctuary, and his bringing game meat into the Sanctuary is not like his bringing the game itself alive into the Sanctuary; for had it been so, he would have been forbidden to bring it in, and its eating in it would have been made like being prevented from hunting in all that, and had he eaten it in the Sanctuary there would have been due upon him what is due for killing game. Since the Sanctuary does not prevent game meat hunted in the lawful land, as it prevents live game, reasoning upon that is that ihram likewise makes live game unlawful for the muhrim, but its meat is not unlawful to him when a non-muhrim undertakes its slaughter, by analogy and reasoning upon what we mentioned of the ruling of the muhrim. So this is the reasoning in this chapter, and it is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them."
"3580","Chapter: Raising the hands upon seeing the House. 3821 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, and from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Hands are raised in seven places: at the opening of prayer, at the House, upon al-Safa and al-Marwa, at Arafat, at Muzdalifa, and at the two pebble pillars."
"3581","3822 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: al-Muharibi narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So this hadith was acted upon; we know no one who opposed anything of it except the raising of the hands at the House, for some people held to that and argued with this hadith, while others opposed them in that and disliked raising the hands upon seeing the House, and they argued in that with what."
"3582","3823 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shuba narrated to us, from Abu Qazaa al-Bahili, from al-Muhajir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, that he was asked about raising the hands at the House, and he said: That is something the Jews do; we performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not do that. So this Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, informs that that is of the deed of the Jews, and not of the deed of the people of Islam, and that they performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not do that. If this chapter is taken by way of chains, then this chain is better than the chain of the first hadith; and if it is taken by way of authenticating the meanings of reports, then Jabir has informed that that is of the deed of the Jews. So it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded it by way of following them, since his ruling was to be upon their law, for they are People of the Book, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, brings for him a law abrogating their law; then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Hajj and opposed them, and did not raise his hands then, out of opposing them. So the hadith of Jabir is more worthy, for in it, along with the authentication of these two hadiths, is the abrogation of the hadith of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. And if it is taken by way of reasoning, then we have seen the raising mentioned in this hadith to be of two kinds: among it raising for the takbir of prayer, and among it raising for supplication. As for what is for prayer, it is raising the hands at the opening of prayer; and as for what is for supplication, it is raising the hands upon al-Safa and al-Marwa, at Jam, at Arafa, and at the two pebble pillars, and this is agreed upon. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also concerning raising the hands at Arafa what."
"3583","3824 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad informed me, from Bishr ibn Harb, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to supplicate at Arafa, and he used to raise his hands toward his chest. So we wanted to consider the raising of the hands upon seeing the House, whether it is so or not. And we saw those who held to that holding that it is not because of ihram, but for venerating the House. And we have seen the raising at Arafa and Muzdalifa, at the two pebble pillars, and upon al-Safa and al-Marwa, to be only commanded by way of supplication in the place in which that standing is made because of ihram. And we have seen one who comes to Arafa, Muzdalifa, the place of stoning, or al-Safa and al-Marwa, while not in ihram, that he does not raise his hands for venerating any of that. Since it is established that raising the hands is not commanded in these places except because of ihram, and it is not commanded in other than ihram, so likewise raising the hands for seeing the House is not commanded in other than ihram. And when it is established that it is not commanded in other than ihram, it is established that it is not commanded in ihram either. And another proof: we have seen what raising the hands is commanded at in ihram to be what one is commanded to stand at of the places we mentioned. And we have seen the Pillar of Aqaba to be a pillar like the other pillars, except that there is no standing at it, so there was no raising there. So reasoning upon that is that the House, since there is no standing at it, there should be no raising at it, by analogy and reasoning upon what we mentioned of that. And this which we have established by reasoning is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them. And there has been narrated in that from Ibrahim al-Nakhai what."
"3584","3825 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Yusuf, may Allah be pleased with him, from Abu Hanifa, may Allah be pleased with him, from Talha ibn Musarrif, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, he said: Hands are raised in seven places: at the opening of prayer, in the takbir for qunut in witr, in the two Eids, at touching the Stone, upon al-Safa and al-Marwa, at Jam and Arafat, and at the two stations at the two pebble pillars. Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: As for at the opening of prayer, in the two Eids, in witr, and at touching the Stone, he turns the backs of his palms toward his face; and as for in the other three, he faces with the insides of his palms toward his face. As for what we mentioned at the opening of prayer, the Muslims have all unanimously agreed upon that. And as for the takbira in qunut in witr, it is an additional takbira in that prayer, and those who perform qunut before bowing have unanimously agreed upon raising with it. So reasoning upon that is that likewise every additional takbira in every prayer; so the additional takbir of the two Eids therein over the rest of prayer is likewise. And as for at touching the Stone, that is made a takbir with which tawaf is opened, as prayer is opened with takbir, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also commanded it."
"3585","3826 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Yafur al-Abdi, he said: I heard a commander who was over Mecca, from the side of al-Hajjaj, in the year seventy-three, saying: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was a strong man, and he used to crowd at the Corner. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Abu Hafs, you are a strong man, and you crowd at the Corner, so you harm the weak; when you see an opening, touch it, otherwise say takbir and pass."
"3586","3827 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awana narrated to us, from Abu Yafur, from a man of Khuzaa, he said: And al-Hajjaj had employed him over Mecca, then he mentioned the like of it. Since that takbir is made that with which tawaf is opened, like the takbir which is made that with which prayer is opened, raising is commanded in it, as raising is commanded in the takbir for opening prayer, especially since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made tawaf of the House a prayer."
"3587","3828 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 3829 - And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, they said: al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Tawaf of the House is a prayer, except that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has permitted speech to you; so whoever speaks, let him speak only good. So this is the cause for which raising is obligatory in what exceeds what is in the first hadith. And as for the raising upon al-Safa and al-Marwa, at Jam, at Arafat, and at the two stations at the two pebble pillars, that has come explicitly stated in the first report. And this which we described of these meanings which we have established is the statement of Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy upon them."
"3588","Chapter: Ramal in tawaf. 3830 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Abu Asim al-Ghanawi, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: Your people claim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ramal at the House, and that that is a Sunnah. He said: They told the truth and they lied. I said: In what did they tell the truth and in what did they lie? He said: They told the truth: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ramal at the House; and they lied: it is not a Sunnah. Quraysh said at the time of Hudaybiyya: Leave Muhammad and his Companions until they die the death of naghaf. Then when they made peace with him that he would come in the next year and stay three days in Mecca, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his Companions arrived, while the polytheists were upon the mountain of Quayqian. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Perform ramal at the House three times, but it is not a Sunnah. Abu Jafar said: So some people held that ramal in tawaf is not a Sunnah, and they argued in that with this hadith, and they said: Ramal was only so that the polytheists would see that they have strength, and that they are not weak, not because that is a Sunnah. And they argued in that also with what."
"3589","3831 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Mecca with his Companions, and the polytheists said: There shall come to you people weakened by the fever of Yathrib. Then when they arrived, the polytheists sat on the side of al-Hijr, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded his Companions to perform ramal in the three circuits, and to walk between the two Corners. Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: And nothing prevented him from commanding them to perform ramal in the four circuits except mercy toward them."
"3590","3832 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Nusayr narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifa narrated to us, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: Your people claim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ramal at the House and that it is a Sunnah. He said: They told the truth and they lied. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ramal at the House, but it is not a Sunnah; rather the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived at Mecca while the polytheists were upon Quayqian, and it reached him that they say: He and his Companions suffer emaciation. So he said to his Companions: Perform ramal, show them that you have strength. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ramal from the Black Stone to the Yamani Corner, and when he was hidden from them he walked. They said: Do you not see that he commanded them to walk in the three circuits between the two Corners where the polytheists do not see them, and commanded them to perform ramal in what remains of these circuits to show them. Since he commanded them with ramal where they see them, and with leaving it where they do not see them, it is established thereby that ramal was because of them, not because it is a Sunnah. They said: And among what proves that is that he did not do that when he performed Hajj, and they mentioned in that what."
"3591","3833 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Qays narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ramal in Umra and walked in Hajj. Do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not perform ramal in his Hajj, where those for whose sake he performed ramal in his Umra were absent. And others opposed them in that and said: Ramal in the first three circuits is a Sunnah, which should not be abandoned in Hajj nor in Umra. And they argued in that with what."
"3592","3834 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Tufayl, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Umra from al-Jirana, and performed ramal at the House three times, and walked four circuits. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ramal in all the circuits, though in some of them the polytheists saw him, and in some they did not see him. So in his ramal where they did not see him is proof that he did not perform ramal because of them, but for another meaning."
"3593","3835 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ramal from the Stone to the Stone. So this hadith is like the one before it."
"3594","3836 - Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to perform ramal from the Stone to the Stone three times, and walk four at his ease. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it."
"3595","3837 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Affan narrated to us, he said: Sulaym ibn Akhdar narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ramal from the Stone to the Stone. So this is also like the one before it. And Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, argued with that for what we mentioned, and did it after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had done it, except that there is not in that that he did it in Hajj nor in Umra. So it is possible that that was from him while he was a pilgrim, so that contradicts what Mujahid narrated from him. And it is possible that that was from him in an Umra, so his doctrine was to perform ramal in Umra and not to perform ramal in Hajj. And among what also proves the establishment of ramal, and that it is a continuing Sunnah in Hajj and Umra, is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did it in the Farewell Hajj, where there was no enemy to show his strength to. So what was narrated from him in that is what."
"3596","3836 (M) - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hastened three and walked four when he arrived in Hajj and Umra, when he had performed Umra."
"3597","3837 (M) - Ismail ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, from Anas ibn Iyad, from Musa ibn Uqba, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with the like of its meaning. So this contradicts what Mujahid narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. And there has been narrated from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he performed ramal in the Farewell Hajj."
"3598","3838 - Muhammad ibn Khuzayma and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) circumambulated in the Farewell Hajj seven times, performing ramal in three of them and walking four."
"3599","3839 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"3600","3840 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik informed him, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) circumambulated seven times, performing ramal in three of them, from the Black Stone to the Black Stone. Since it is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he performed ramal in the Farewell Hajj, while there was no enemy, it is established that he did not do it only when enmity was because of the enemy; and had he done it then because of them, he would not have done it at the time of their absence. So it is established thereby that ramal in tawaf is of the performed Sunan of Hajj therein, which should not be abandoned. And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after him also did that."
